\hypertarget{autodock-vina}{%
\subsubsection{AutoDock Vina}\label{autodock-vina}}

AutoDock Vina is a physics-based docking program that couples an efficient global search engine to an interchangeable empirical scoring function \cite{ref022}, \cite{ref023}. It helps to keep the two components apart, because the engine that samples and refines candidate poses is largely independent of the scoring function that ranks them, and the same engine can be driven by more than one such function. Two are relevant here, namely the original Vina function and the later Vinardo function, both selectable within the same program \cite{ref023}. After receptor and ligand preparation, the engine generates random poses inside a user-defined search box and refines them by iterated local search, namely random perturbation followed by gradient-based local optimisation with a BFGS-like quasi-Newton method over precomputed interaction grids. Similar poses are clustered by RMSD and the lowest-energy representatives are returned \cite{ref023}. Because the chosen scoring function is evaluated together with its gradient, each pose is minimised against it during the search, so no separate post-docking optimisation is needed.

The default Vina scoring function expresses the predicted binding free energy as a weighted sum of pairwise atom-type terms over the surface distance between atoms, namely two attractive Gaussian steric terms, a repulsive parabola, a hydrophobic term and a hydrogen-bond term, divided by a factor growing with the number of rotatable bonds as a flexibility penalty. Its six weights were fixed once by tuning against the PDBbind set rather than derived from first principles, giving Vina a hybrid empirical and knowledge-based character \cite{ref022}. The first Gaussian together with the repulsion term approximates the attractive well and steep wall of a dispersion interaction without its hard singularity, while the second, longer-range Gaussian adds a broader attraction further from contact, and this smooth steric model stays well suited to fast local optimisation.

Vinardo, short for Vina RaDii Optimized, is a reparametrised and deliberately simpler variant of this function \cite{ref073}. It keeps the same families of terms but drops the second, longer-range Gaussian, which in Vina introduced a second steric minimum about 3\angstrom{} beyond atomic contact and tended to seat ligands too deeply in the pocket. Vinardo also re-optimises the atomic radii, enlarging that of aliphatic carbon while shrinking those of nitrogen and oxygen, and re-fits the remaining term weights. These parameters were selected by screening candidate functions on the RMSD of crystallographic poses after local minimisation and then confirming the best by actual redocking, rather than by maximising correlation with measured affinities, on the finding that a good affinity fit does not by itself yield good docking behaviour \cite{ref073}. The authors report that the resulting function improves scoring, docking and virtual-screening performance relative to Vina at a lower parameter count \cite{ref073}.

Switching from Vina to Vinardo therefore changes only the objective that the shared engine minimises and ranks, not the search machinery itself, which keeps the two directly comparable within AutoDock Vina. The full protocol, scoring equations and fitted weights for both functions are reproduced in the Appendix (Tools and Functions, AutoDock Vina).

\hypertarget{ai-based-molecular-docking}{%
\subsection{AI-Based Molecular Docking}\label{ai-based-molecular-docking}}

AI-based molecular docking tools employ diverse computational strategies. Common strategies include deep-learning neural networks that predict binding poses directly from protein and ligand structures, diffusion models like DiffDock \cite{ref024} that treat docking as a generative sampling problem, geometric deep learning approaches such as EquiBind \cite{ref009}, \cite{ref010}, which predicts the bound pose directly with an SE(3)-equivariant network, and TankBind \cite{ref025}, \cite{ref026}, which encodes the protein with an equivariant network but predicts an inter-molecular distance map through a trigonometry module and then derives coordinates from it by optimisation, and regression-based models like KarmaDock \cite{ref027} and GAABind \cite{ref028} that predict binding affinities or poses with learned scoring functions. Other notable AI-based algorithms include SurfDock, DiffBindFR, DynamicBind, QuickBind, and hybrid methods like Interformer, which integrate traditional conformational searches with AI-driven scoring functions. Some methods, such as DeepDock and Uni-Mol, perform "blind docking" without requiring predefined binding sites, while others, like co-folding models, simultaneously predict structural changes in both protein and ligand to account for flexibility \cite{ref013}, \cite{ref029}.

In contrast to traditional exhaustive conformational search algorithms and empirical scoring functions, AI-based tools learn complex interaction patterns from large datasets of protein-ligand complexes to directly predict optimal binding conformations. Moreover, AI-based methods tend to be computationally efficient, which improves scalability for large-scale virtual screening \cite{ref051}.

\hypertarget{diffdock}{%
\subsubsection{DiffDock}\label{diffdock}}

DiffDock is a diffusion generative model that treats pose prediction as a generative task rather than optimisation of a hand-crafted energy function \cite{ref024}. Protein and ligand are represented as graphs, and the ligand pose is parameterised by translation, rotation and internal torsion angles. During training a forward process corrupts known poses by adding noise to these variables, and at inference the reverse process starts from random placements and denoises them over several steps, where an SE(3)-equivariant neural network predicts updates that move the pose toward a high-probability bound conformation. The equivariance keeps predictions consistent under rigid motions of the coordinates. Because sampling is stochastic, DiffDock returns several plausible poses, which suits multimodal docking landscapes. Its scoring has two learned components, namely a diffusion score model that steers the denoising trajectory and a confidence model that ranks poses by predicted correctness, that is whether a pose falls below a 2\angstrom{} RMSD threshold rather than an affinity-like energy. These criteria live implicitly in the network weights, so performance depends heavily on the training data and confidence-model calibration \cite{ref024}. Full details are given in the Appendix (Tools and Functions, DiffDock).

\hypertarget{equibind}{%
\subsubsection{EquiBind}\label{equibind}}

EquiBind is a direct geometric deep-learning approach to blind docking predicting the bound pose directly in a single forward pass of an SE(3)-equivariant neural network. Protein and ligand are encoded as graphs, and interaction-aware equivariant message passing between them lets ligand atoms attend to the most relevant protein regions, so the network infers both where the ligand binds and how it is oriented without a predefined site. Its central mechanism is the prediction of matching key points on the ligand and the protein surface, after which the best rigid-body transformation is computed by a singular-value-decomposition (Kabsch-style) step that places the ligand into the pocket, followed by a conformer-fitting stage that adjusts torsions. EquiBind uses no separate scoring function at inference, and is instead trained with losses that penalise the geometric error to the experimental reference, namely ligand RMSD-type, alignment and region losses, so the ranking rule is internalised during training and no affinity-like number is produced. Dropping both stochastic search and repeated denoising gives it a large runtime advantage for high-throughput docking. Full details are given in the Appendix (Tools and Functions, EquiBind).

\hypertarget{optimization-tools}{%
\subsection{Optimisation Tools}\label{optimization-tools}}

\hypertarget{smina}{%
\subsubsection{smina}\label{smina}}

Unlike the tools above, smina and gnina are used here as lightweight refinement tools that re-score and locally optimise generated poses. smina is a fork of AutoDock Vina developed by Koes et al. \cite{ref055}. It inherits Vina\textquotesingle s empirical scoring function and search machinery unchanged but re-implements them so that individual energy terms can be reweighted or swapped and custom atom types defined. The energy it optimises is therefore the Vina objective itself, namely the weighted sum of two Gaussian steric terms, a steric-repulsion penalty, a hydrophobic contact term and a hydrogen-bond term, divided by a factor growing with the number of rotatable bonds, the same function described above. Beyond full docking, smina exposes a fast local-minimisation mode that relaxes a supplied pose into the nearest energy minimum by gradient-based optimisation without repeating the conformational search. This makes it well suited to post-docking optimisation, since a pose seated in roughly the correct pocket but clashing with the receptor can be minimised against the Vina function, removing the clash while leaving the placement essentially unchanged \cite{ref057}.

\hypertarget{gnina}{%
\subsubsection{gnina}\label{gnina}}

Because gnina is a fork of smina, both share the docking algorithm inherited from Vina \cite{ref056}, \cite{ref067}, \cite{ref068}. Sampling uses Markov-chain Monte Carlo chains that apply random rigid-body and torsional perturbations, drive each candidate to a local minimum of the Vina energy by quasi-Newton minimisation, and accept or reject it by a Metropolis criterion, so the Vina energy is the quantity optimised during search. gnina differs in ranking, replacing the hand-crafted score with a learned one. An ensemble of convolutional neural networks rescores the poses, each reading a three-dimensional grid of Gaussian atom-type densities built with libmolgrid \cite{ref076} and trained on two objectives, namely a cross-entropy classifier that separates poses within 2\angstrom{} RMSD of the native mode from poses outside it (CNNscore) and a regression head that predicts a binding constant on a pK scale and is hinged for poses judged inaccurate (CNNaffinity) \cite{ref075}, \cite{ref056}, \cite{ref067}. Their product CNN\_VS is preferred for virtual screening \cite{ref068}. gnina therefore couples a physics-based sampler to a deep-learning scoring function \cite{ref058}. Full details appear in the Appendix (Tools and Functions, Optimisation Tools).

\hypertarget{gnina-rescoring-and-gnina-refinement}{%
\subsubsection{gnina Rescoring and gnina Refinement}\label{gnina-rescoring-and-gnina-refinement}}

gnina can be applied to an existing pose in two ways that are easy to conflate but are not the same operation, and this work uses both. The difference lies in what the learned score is permitted to change. In the first mode the network only reorders poses, while in the second it also determines where their atoms end up \cite{ref056}, \cite{ref058}.

Rescoring is the mode gnina applies by default. The pose is first driven to a local minimum of the empirical function by the inherited quasi-Newton minimiser, and only once that minimisation has finished are the convolutional networks evaluated, after which the pose set is reordered by the learned score \cite{ref056}. The network therefore changes the ranking of a set of poses and not the geometry of any one of them. Coordinates do shift relative to the input, because a local minimisation was performed, but that displacement is produced by the empirical objective in exactly the way smina would produce it. Rescoring is the cheapest way to use the model and is the configuration in which gnina has been most broadly validated, including the prospective CACHE screen in which it identified experimentally confirmed hits \cite{ref068}.

Refinement inverts this. The learned score becomes the objective of the final local minimisation, so the network decides the geometry. This is possible because the atom-density grid the network reads is constructed to be differentiable with respect to the input coordinates, which allows the score to be back-propagated into per-atom forces that then drive the same quasi-Newton minimiser \cite{ref074}, \cite{ref076}. In this mode the empirical function is still evaluated and still reported, but it does not enter the objective unless it is explicitly mixed back in, which is not the default behaviour \cite{ref058}. A further asymmetry is worth stating, because it is not obvious from the option name. The quantity that supplies the gradient is the pose-classification head, so refinement moves atoms toward higher CNNscore, whereas the ordering of the refined poses in this work is taken from CNNaffinity. The optimised criterion and the ranking criterion are therefore not the same number.

The published assessment of the two modes is asymmetric. The gnina authors report that refinement costs roughly an order of magnitude more computation than rescoring while performing no better, and they explicitly recommend against it, which is why rescoring remains the shipped default \cite{ref056}. Earlier prospective work on convolutional-network minimisation found it useful on some targets without being uniformly better than minimisation against the empirical function \cite{ref077}. The broader literature supplies a reason to expect this asymmetry. A learned score is fitted to discriminate poses rather than to reproduce a potential energy surface, and machine-learned scoring functions have been shown to degrade only gradually when a ligand is deliberately pushed into the protein instead of rising steeply as an explicit repulsion term does \cite{ref078}. Frameworks that do optimise coordinates against a learned score generally recombine it with a traditional physics term for precisely that reason \cite{ref085}, \cite{ref053}, and deep-learning docking output is in general more prone to failing physical-plausibility checks than the output of classical methods \cite{ref035}, \cite{ref013}.

The consequence for this study is that the two modes answer different questions and are therefore never merged into a single gnina variant. Rescoring supplies the deep-learning pipelines with the geometric repair and the ranking they do not otherwise provide. Refinement is applied to the AutoDock variants as a direct test of the recommendation above, because poses that enter it are already almost entirely valid, which isolates what the network does to geometry from what an empirical minimisation would have repaired anyway. The measured effect of each mode is reported in the Results, and the protocols are set out in the Appendix (Docking Protocols, AutoDock Vina).

