Limited receptor flexibility was represented with four supplied Orai1 molecular-dynamics snapshots, namely Fr0, Fr300, Fr400 and Fr499 \cite{ref038}, \cite{ref072}. No pH-based protonation was applied to them. The snapshots arrive with CHARMM hydrogens and CHARMM histidine states, receptor preparation strips every hydrogen and collapses HSD, HSE and HSP to plain HIS, and the PDBQT converter then re-adds polar hydrogens by its own geometric rules, so the receptor protonation reported here is neither inherited from the molecular-dynamics model nor titrated at a stated pH. The selected ligands were prepared for a nominal pH of 7.0. Both protonation states of GSK-7975A were prepared and docked, and the Orai1 analyses reported in this thesis use the neutral form, which is the species carried through the transmembrane filter, the validity report, the pose clustering and the interaction analysis. The descriptors above are those of that neutral species (C\textsubscript{18}H\textsubscript{12}F\textsubscript{5}N\textsubscript{3}O\textsubscript{2}, 397.3~Da), and Table~\ref{tab:appendix-orai-descriptors} tabulates the same species, so the two tables and the reported results now describe one consistent ligand. For reference the deprotonated form is C\textsubscript{18}H\textsubscript{11}F\textsubscript{5}N\textsubscript{3}O\textsubscript{2}\textsuperscript{\ensuremath{-}} at 396.3~Da. The deprotonated input file was corrected during this work. Its earlier preparation had removed the phenol proton from the three-dimensional structure without assigning the compensating negative charge, so the file described a neutral radical with a dearomatised ring rather than the phenolate. It has been rebuilt as the phenolate with its heavy-atom geometry preserved exactly, and the deprotonated form does not enter any result reported here. 2-APB and Synta-66 were docked as neutral species.

The experimental ligand set contains three Orai-related chemotypes, namely 2-APB, docked in its neutral protonation state and labelled 2abp-NH2 in the per-compound results tables, together with GSK-7975A \cite{ref041}, \cite{ref070} and Synta-66 \cite{ref071}. The docked structure is native 2-APB (2-aminoethoxydiphenyl borate), so the reported 2-APB pharmacology \cite{ref040} characterises the docked molecule directly. GSK-7975A was docked in its neutral form, which is the species whose descriptors appear in Table~\ref{tab:methods-experimental-ligands} and in Table~\ref{tab:appendix-orai-descriptors} and which underlies every Orai1 result reported here. A deprotonated phenolate form was also prepared during ligand setup and is retained in the deposited input files under a name carrying the suffix deprot, but it enters no analysis in this thesis and its descriptors differ from the docked species, having one fewer hydrogen-bond donor and a molecular weight of 396.3\,Da. No cognate Orai1 crystal pose is available for any of the docked compounds, so this set supports a reference-free case study rather than a conventional accuracy benchmark.

All three compounds are tool compounds for store-operated calcium entry, that is the STIM1 to Orai1 pathway that opens calcium release-activated calcium channels once the endoplasmic store is depleted, yet they act on it in three different ways, which is what makes the set informative for a docking comparison.

GSK-7975A and Synta-66 act at or near the extracellular pore mouth and the selectivity filter rather than in the conduction pathway, so for those two a chemically valid pose lodged inside the transmembrane slab is implausible. 2-APB is carried here as a compound whose site is not experimentally resolved and whose documented STIM1 arm is cytosolic, so the same expectation cannot be asserted for it on structural grounds. The placement criterion used in the results chapter therefore applies one operational study assumption uniformly to all three compounds rather than an established fact about each of them. Their differing scaffolds also load the three docking tools unevenly. The full pharmacology of each compound, namely GSK-7975A, Synta-66 and 2-APB (docked in its neutral form, 2abp-NH2), together with the sites, selectivity liabilities and mechanistic differences that make the biologically defensible region non-identical across the set, is set out in Appendix~\ref{experimental-ligand-pharmacology}.

Docking each of the four receptor snapshots with each of the three drug compounds yields 12 frame--ligand units. Each unit was modelled with every toolchain to allow comparison of the resulting poses.

\hypertarget{evaluation-metrics}{%
\section{Evaluation Metrics}\label{evaluation-metrics}}

Assessing generated poses requires three complementary questions. First, is the ligand placed in a receptor region capable of the interactions relevant to the pharmacological hypothesis? Second, does the ligand retain a credible internal conformation and fit within that region? Third, is the resulting protein-ligand complex physically and chemically admissible? The metrics below address placement, form and validity separately before combining them.

\hypertarget{near-nativeness-rmsd-2uxe5}{%
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Near-Nativeness (RMSD \ensuremath{\leq} 2\angstrom{})}{Near-Nativeness (RMSD <= 2 Angstrom)}}\label{near-nativeness-rmsd-2uxe5}}

The docking results are compared on three primary measures, applied identically to every tool. The first is geometric accuracy. It is the symmetry-corrected heavy-atom root-mean-square deviation (RMSD) between a docked pose and the crystallographic ligand, defined as

\[\text{RMSD=}\sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i = 1}^{N}d_{i}^{\text{2}}}{N}}\]

where N is the number of heavy atoms and d\textsubscript{i} is the Euclidean distance between the i-th pair of corresponding atoms in their docked and crystallographic positions, computed in place without superposing the pose onto the crystal ligand. A pose counts as accurate at the conventional threshold of RMSD \ensuremath{\leq} 2\angstrom{} \cite{ref022}, \cite{ref050}. The symmetry correction matters because many benchmark ligands are wholly or partly symmetric, so a naïve atom mapping would overstate the error, and it is computed by graph-isomorphism atom mapping over the symmetry-equivalent assignments \cite{ref044}, \cite{ref048}. Because a geometrically close pose can still be chemically or sterically impossible, accuracy is paired with physical plausibility assessed by the PoseBusters battery (PB-valid), described below \cite{ref035}.

The two are combined into a single valid-near-native criterion, namely RMSD \ensuremath{\leq} 2\angstrom{} and PB-valid, which is the headline metric throughout the crystal-referenced calibration chapters and separates a pose that merely looks right from one that could be used. It is deliberately kept distinct in name from the operational usable-pose rule defined for Orai1 below, which is RMSD-free because that target has no crystal ligand to measure against. The 2\angstrom{} threshold is the established standard in the docking literature and the AutoDock Vina benchmark, keeping the results comparable with published work \cite{ref022}, \cite{ref035}, \cite{ref050}.

Because a tool can generate a near-native pose yet fail to rank it first, accuracy is reported under two selection regimes, namely the realistic top-1 pose and the oracle (best) pose, the most accurate pose anywhere in the tool's output. The gap between them isolates ranking quality from sampling quality and is developed through the per-rank success rate and the cumulative recovery within the first k ranked poses. A single top-1 view would conflate a weak confidence model with weak sampling and systematically understate the deep-learning tools.

Two secondary measures complete the comparison. Binding-site recovery asks the coarser question of whether a tool places the ligand in the correct pocket at all, assessed by clustering the docked poses across tools and against the crystallographic pocket, which is the appropriate resolution for the reference-free Orai1 system. Computational cost is reported as wall-clock time and as core-seconds per qualifying pose, that is per pose that is PoseBusters-valid and within 2\angstrom{} of the crystal ligand, since practical value for large-scale screening depends on qualifying poses returned per unit of compute.

\hypertarget{form-kabsch-rmsd-1-uxe5}{%
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Form (Kabsch RMSD \ensuremath{\leq} 1\angstrom{})}{Form (Kabsch RMSD <= 1 Angstrom)}}\label{form-kabsch-rmsd-1-uxe5}}

Geometric accuracy scored as in-place RMSD, the quantity the \ensuremath{\leq} 2\angstrom{} gate uses, conflates two independent errors, namely where the molecule sits and what shape it holds. The approximate decomposition in-place\textsuperscript{2} \ensuremath{\approx} placement\textsuperscript{2} + form\textsuperscript{2} separates them, the form error being the best-fit (Kabsch) RMSD that remains once translation and rotation are removed and the placement error the residual diagnostic obtained from that relation. A pose is called form-correct when its form error is \ensuremath{\leq} 1\angstrom{}.

The heavy-atom RMSD remaining after this best fit is the form error, the component of the deviation that no repositioning can remove, that is the discrepancy in the ligand's own three-dimensional shape. With both ligands translated to a common centroid and R the optimal proper rotation obtained by the Kabsch singular-value-decomposition construction \cite{ref062}, it is

\[\text{RMSD}_{\text{form}}\text{=}\sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i = 1}^{N}\left\| Rx_{i}\text{-}y_{i} \right\|^{\text{2}}}{N}}\]

where x\textsubscript{i} and y\textsubscript{i} are the centred coordinates of the i-th heavy-atom pair and N the number of heavy atoms. As with the in-place RMSD, the minimisation is taken over the ligand's symmetry-equivalent atom mappings so that chemically equivalent atoms are not spuriously penalised \cite{ref044}, and the value is read from the PoseBusters implementation \cite{ref035}.

% RESOLVED 2026-08-16: the analysis code does not share one mapping, since the in-place value comes from RDKit CalcRMS and the Kabsch value from GetBestRMS (posebusters/modules/rmsd.py), each minimising over symmetry mappings independently, so the decomposition is described here as approximate.
For a fixed atom correspondence, rigid superposition gives the per-pose decomposition in-place\textsuperscript{2} = placement\textsuperscript{2} + form\textsuperscript{2}, and placement can be derived as \(\sqrt{\text{in-place}^{2}-\text{form}^{2}}\). That correspondence is not shared by the two terms as they are computed here, because the in-place and best-fit values are each minimised over the symmetry-equivalent atom mappings independently, so the two minimisations may settle on different mappings for the same pose. The decomposition is therefore approximate rather than an identity, and the placement value is reported as a residual diagnostic rather than as an independently measured component. Form recovery is reported for PoseBusters-valid outputs independently of the 2 \angstrom{} in-place threshold, and a pose is form-correct at Kabsch RMSD \ensuremath{\leq} 1 \angstrom{}.

This separation matters because binding is mediated by specific directional contacts, namely hydrogen bonds, hydrophobic burial, \ensuremath{\pi}-stacking and salt bridges, whose geometry is set by the ligand's bound conformation, so a pose reproduces the native interaction pattern only if its form is right. Isolating form therefore probes the physical prerequisite for the interactions the docking is meant to predict, and it localises the limiting stage, since a placement-limited pose (right shape, wrong position) implicates pocket targeting while a form-limited pose (wrong conformation) implicates conformer generation or refinement. The tool-by-tool analysis is presented in the Results (Figures~\ref{fig:results-r2a} to \ref{fig:results-r3}).

\hypertarget{physical-and-chemical-validity-posebusters-checks}{%
\subsection{Physical and Chemical Validity (PoseBusters Checks)}\label{physical-and-chemical-validity-posebusters-checks}}

Geometric and form closeness to the crystal do not by themselves guarantee that a pose is physically possible, since RMSD alone can be satisfied by chemically distorted or sterically clashing poses, the failure mode the original PoseBusters study attributes to AI-based methods \cite{ref035}. Every pose was therefore run through the PoseBusters ``dock'' battery and is counted PB-valid only if it passes all twenty-two applicable PoseBusters checks (Table~\ref{tab:appendix-posebusters-checks}). The battery also defines four further checks that compare a pose against a co-crystallised reference ligand, namely the reference-dependent identity tests on molecular formula, bond graph, tetrahedral chirality and double-bond stereochemistry. No reference ligand was supplied to the checker in any run of this thesis, including the calibration benchmark, so those four checks were not applicable and were not executed, and their result columns are absent from every result file. PB-valid therefore denotes the twenty-two reference-free checks in all three datasets, which keeps the criterion identical for the crystal-referenced calibration set and the reference-free Orai1 case study.

The checks fall into three groups. The first covers chemical validity, namely that the pose file and the receptor file parse into RDKit molecules, that the ligand passes chemical sanitisation and converts to a standard InChI string, that all its atoms form a single connected component and that it carries no radical atoms. Because the four reference-dependent checks were not run, PB-valid does not certify that the molecular formula, the bond graph or the tetrahedral and double-bond stereochemistry of a docked pose still match the ligand that was submitted to the tool. The second covers intramolecular geometry, namely bond lengths and angles within distance-geometry tolerance, flat aromatic rings and double bonds, absence of internal steric clash and no gross conformational strain. The third covers intermolecular validity against the receptor, namely minimum distances to the protein, to organic and inorganic cofactors and to waters together with the corresponding volume overlaps, so that the ligand neither interpenetrates the receptor nor occupies space already taken. The full list of individual checks and their tolerances is given in the Appendix (Table~\ref{tab:appendix-posebusters-checks}).

\hypertarget{operational-placement-transmembrane-exclusion}{%
\subsection{Operational Placement (Transmembrane Exclusion)}\label{operational-placement-transmembrane-exclusion}}

The Orai1 target has no crystallographic ligand, so near-nativeness cannot be scored and the accuracy criterion is replaced by an operational placement rule. A pose counts as usable when it is PoseBusters-valid, placed on the receptor and outside the transmembrane exclusion slab defined below. This subsection states both conditions geometrically, because together they gate every Orai1 quantity reported in this thesis.

Placed on the receptor is a physical-contact gate rather than a pocket criterion, and it exists because a generative model occasionally emits a rigid-body-translated pose that is internally intact yet sits hundreds or thousands of \angstrom{} away from the protein. The test is applied to the ligand heavy atoms of the pose as written, with hydrogens ignored. A pose counts as placed when three conditions hold at once. Its coordinates must be readable and finite, with no atom further than 10\textsuperscript{4}\angstrom{} from the origin. The PoseBusters protein-ligand maximum-distance check, which requires the ligand to lie within the interaction range of the receptor, must pass, this verdict being authoritative wherever the column is present. And the heavy-atom centroid must fall inside a generous receptor envelope, namely a radial distance of at most 80\angstrom{} from the pore axis defined below and an axial fraction between \ensuremath{-}4 and 5 of the slab frame, where the axial fraction is the axial coordinate divided by the slab thickness so that 0 and 1 are the two ring planes. The envelope is deliberately far wider than the hexamer, whose outer protein shell reaches 32 to 36\angstrom{} from the axis, so it removes only translated artefacts and never a pose in contact with the protein. Poses failing the gate are recorded as invalid placement and enter no reported count.

Orai1 is a hexamer whose pore is lined by the TM1 helix of each of the six subunits. Along TM1 the pore runs from the cytosolic basic residue Arg91 to the extracellular selectivity filter Glu106, so the band of the structure lying between those two residue rings is the membrane-spanning region, which conducts ions rather than presenting a druggable surface pocket. The slab is built from that pair of rings. For a given receptor frame let \(p_{\text{R91}}\) be the centroid of the six Arg91 C\ensuremath{\alpha} atoms, one per subunit, and \(p_{\text{E106}}\) the centroid of the six Glu106 C\ensuremath{\alpha} atoms. These define the pore axis

\[\hat{a} = \frac{p_{\text{E106}} - p_{\text{R91}}}{\left\| p_{\text{E106}} - p_{\text{R91}} \right\|},\qquad L = \left\| p_{\text{E106}} - p_{\text{R91}} \right\|\]

and for any point \(x\) the axial coordinate is \(t(x) = (x - p_{\text{R91}}) \cdot \hat{a}\), so that \(t = 0\) is the Arg91 plane and \(t = L\) the Glu106 plane. The exclusion slab is the region

\[\left\{ x : 0 \leq t(x) \leq L \right\}\]

that is the set of points lying between the two ring planes, taken with no padding beyond either plane. A pose is flagged as transmembrane when the centroid of its ligand heavy atoms falls inside the slab, and it is removed from the usable count. The fraction of ligand atoms inside the slab and a majority-of-atoms flag are recorded alongside the centroid test so that a stricter atom-level rule can be applied without recomputing the geometry.

Two properties of this definition matter for interpretation. First, the slab is bounded only along the pore axis and is unbounded radially, so a ligand resting against the lipid-facing outer surface of the hexamer is excluded just as a ligand in the conduction pathway is. This is deliberate, since both positions sit within the membrane band and neither is a solvent-accessible surface pocket. The pore lumen itself is far narrower than the excluded region, with a mean backbone radius of 7.0 to 7.7\angstrom{} across the four frames against an outer protein shell reaching 32 to 36\angstrom{} from the axis in the same band. Second, because the four Orai1 snapshots are different conformations in different coordinate systems, the slab is rebuilt for each frame from the exact receptor file that frame\textquotesingle s poses were docked into, so poses and slab always share a coordinate system. The resulting axial thickness is stable across frames at 22.4 to 22.8\angstrom{}.

The rule is an operational hypothesis rather than ground truth. It encodes the pharmacological premise that GSK-7975A and Synta-66 act at or near the extracellular pore mouth and the selectivity filter rather than inside the conduction pathway, and it extends the same expectation to 2-APB, whose site is not experimentally resolved. It cannot establish that an excluded pose is biologically wrong.

\hypertarget{clustering}{%
\subsection{Clustering}\label{clustering}}

Because a predicted pocket from fpocket or P2Rank carries no ligand atoms, the only quantity comparable across docked poses, predicted pockets and the crystallographic site is the three-dimensional centre, so clustering is performed on each pose\textquotesingle s heavy-atom centroid rather than on full-atom RMSD, with RMSD retained as a complementary pose-quality measure. For every complex the centroids of all three tools are pooled into a pairwise Euclidean distance matrix and partitioned by complete-linkage agglomerative clustering cut at 8\angstrom{}, the physical scale of a binding pocket. The number of clusters is discovered rather than fixed and may be one, so a complex on which all three tools genuinely converge is reported as a single site. Complete linkage guarantees that no cluster\textquotesingle s internal spread exceeds 8\angstrom{} and the cut is deterministic. This replaces the earlier silhouette-maximising K-medoids default, which could never return a single cluster and so split converged sites into spurious sub-pockets.

Candidate pockets within a complex are ranked by cross-tool agreement rather than by raw pose count, which stops a high-throughput tool from winning a site on pose count alone. That consensus rule is compared against four alternative prospective rules, including the legacy pose-count rule, in Figure~\ref{fig:results-r6}.

For the reference-free Orai target the same 8\angstrom{} centroid clustering is applied within each MD frame, but because the four snapshots occupy different coordinate systems poses cannot be clustered by absolute position across frames. Each pose is instead represented by the receptor residues within 4\angstrom{} of the ligand, folded over Orai\textquotesingle s six-fold symmetry, so that poses from different frames remain comparable without superposition. With no crystal to arbitrate, the consensus site is defined as the geometric median of the per-tool sites, one point per tool. Cross-tool agreement is then reported as the Euclidean distance between two tools\textquotesingle{} consensus sites within a frame-ligand unit, and a pair counts as agreeing when that distance is at most 5\angstrom{}. The threshold is the same physical reasoning as the 8\angstrom{} clustering cut, namely that two sites separated by less than the radius of a binding pocket are the same site, and it is deliberately more permissive than any pose-level RMSD criterion because it compares pocket centres rather than atom positions.

Cluster quality is assessed on three complementary axes. Silhouette, Calinski-Harabasz, Davies-Bouldin and nearest-cluster separation are defined only for complexes with at least two clusters and are therefore omitted for one-cluster cases. Compactness can be reported for a single cluster, while bootstrap Jaccard overlap and adjusted Rand indices quantify reproducibility under resampling and against independent partitions. Crystal-site recovery is evaluated separately with the prespecified 4 \angstrom{} centroid threshold. The cluster nearest the crystal is not automatically counted as recovered. Both the recovery rates under that threshold and the internal, stability and ranking-rule results are reported with Figures~\ref{fig:results-r5} and \ref{fig:results-r6}.

\hypertarget{statistical-analysis}{%
\subsection{Statistical Analysis}\label{statistical-analysis}}

Every inferential statement in the Results rests on the conventions set out here. All tests are two-sided and are read at a nominal \ensuremath{\alpha} of 0.05. Inferential comparisons are accompanied by an effect size or by the underlying paired proportions or counts, as appropriate, so that the size of a difference can be separated from the confidence attached to it. The tests are distribution-free throughout, because per-complex yields, RMSD values and contact counts are bounded, skewed and frequently zero-inflated, so no normality assumption is made anywhere.

The inferential unit differs by design and is fixed before any test is run. For the calibration benchmark it is the complex, so the poses of one complex are first reduced to one value per complex per tool, either a per-complex yield, a per-complex best value or a per-complex binary recovery indicator. For the Orai1 system there is no crystal reference and each ligand is docked into four molecular-dynamics frames, so the unit is the frame-by-ligand pair. That unit removes the nesting of poses inside a docking run, but it does not make the units independent, because the four frames of a given ligand are snapshots of one receptor and remain correlated with one another. The effective number of independent experimental observations is therefore three, namely the three ligands. Orai1 cross-panel contrasts are for that reason never advanced as established findings. They are computed and corrected within the declared families below, and their adjusted p-values are reported so that the reader can see which would and would not survive, but the conclusion drawn from them is descriptive in every case, and every such contrast is re-tested at the ligand unit in a ligand-level sensitivity analysis reported with the accompanying results. Figures may display pose-level distributions for readability, but wherever a figure shows poses and the text quotes a test, that test was computed on the aggregated unit, and both values are then given side by side.

Most benchmark comparisons are paired, because the same complexes pass through every tool and every ranking depth. Paired binary outcomes such as recovery at a given depth, the existence of at least one PoseBusters-valid pose or crystal-cluster reach are tested with Cochran\textquotesingle s Q as the omnibus across three or more conditions and with the exact McNemar test on the discordant pairs for each pair of conditions, with Wilson score 95\% intervals on the underlying proportions. Paired continuous outcomes such as per-complex yields, RMSD medians and pose counts use the Friedman test with Kendall\textquotesingle s W as its effect size for the omnibus and the Wilcoxon signed-rank test with the matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation for the pairs, with Hodges-Lehmann estimates for the median paired difference. Unpaired comparisons arise only where the two groups are different molecules or different ligand panels, chiefly the Orai1 experimental ligands against the benchmark ligands docked into the same frames. These use the Mann-Whitney U test with Cliff\textquotesingle s \ensuremath{\delta} as the effect size, oriented explicitly in the text, or Fisher\textquotesingle s exact test for pooled two-by-two counts, with the Kruskal-Wallis test and \ensuremath{\varepsilon}\textsuperscript{2} for three-group comparisons and the Jonckheere-Terpstra test where an ordered trend is hypothesised. Monotone association between two per-complex quantities, for instance a tool\textquotesingle s own rank against pose quality, is measured by Kendall\textquotesingle s \ensuremath{\tau}-b or Spearman\textquotesingle s \ensuremath{\rho} per complex and then tested with a one-sample Wilcoxon signed-rank test against zero. Where a paired binary contrast returns no significant difference and that null result carries weight in the argument, the contrast is additionally reported as a difference of correlated proportions with a Newcombe hybrid score interval, and its resolution is characterised by the number of discordant pairs, the minimum detectable difference at 80\% power and a pair of one-sided tests against a stated equivalence margin. This is applied once, to the calibration contrast between the two leading pipelines, and it is the only place in this thesis where an equivalence claim is made. Everywhere else a non-significant result is reported as an absence of detected difference and nothing more.

Multiplicity is corrected within explicitly declared families rather than across the thesis as a whole. Small families use Holm-Bonferroni step-down adjustment, and adjusted values are labelled p\_holm or Holm p. They are the three tool-versus-tool contrasts within one metric, the depth contrasts within one tool, the variant contrasts within one family, the per-tool experimental-versus-control contrasts within one Orai1 metric, which is the three-test family behind the per-unit yield tests and behind the total-contacts test, the eighteen tool-by-metric cluster-quality cells, the four consensus-site contrasts, namely the three tool pairs and their pooled summary and the fifteen per-residue contact-frequency contrasts within one tool, which is the family behind the residue hot-spot test. Large screening families use the Benjamini-Hochberg false-discovery-rate procedure, and adjusted values are labelled q. They are the per-check PoseBusters sweep, the per-descriptor sweeps, the fifteen interaction-type contrasts within one tool, which is the family behind the interaction-type profile and which is corrected separately for each tool rather than pooled over the thirty cells of both mature tools, and the nine tool-by-metric rank-correlation cells. A condition that supplies too few units to test, such as EquiBind with a single usable experimental pose, is left out of its family rather than entered as a hypothesis, so the family shrinks accordingly. Ranking depths are nested cumulative pools rather than independent hypotheses, so in the body they are not treated as a further multiplicity factor and each depth is corrected within its own family, which is stated at the point of use. The appendix recovery chapter departs from that convention deliberately and says so, correcting its six prespecified between-variant comparisons jointly across all four depths as one 24-test family, which is the more conservative choice and is why an effect can be significant there at one depth and not at another. Uncorrected values are reported only where they are named as such.

Resampling is used where no closed-form test applies. Bootstrap confidence intervals for medians, effect sizes and cluster stability use 2,000 resamples with a fixed seed, resampling the inferential unit rather than individual poses so that within-complex correlation is preserved, and the bootstrap Jaccard index quantifying cluster reproducibility uses 25 resamples per complex. Permutation tests, namely the paired permutation test and the three-way cross-tool co-occurrence test, use 10,000 permutations with a fixed seed and report the add-one-adjusted p-value, whose floor is therefore quoted as below 1e-4 rather than as zero.

Missing and dropped cases are handled by listwise deletion on the analysed unit, and the surviving count is stated wherever it differs from the headline sample. A complex that produced no pose for one tool cannot enter a paired comparison involving that tool, a per-complex correlation is defined only when the complex contributes at least three poses with non-constant values, and a per-complex proportion is defined only when the complex produced at least one pose. Degenerate pairs, for instance two conditions whose paired differences are all zero, return an undefined p-value that is carried through the correction as undefined rather than being counted as a hypothesis.

Two caveats stand over the whole analysis. First, the four Orai1 frames of a given ligand are conformational snapshots of one receptor, so the twelve frame-by-ligand units are correlated rather than independent replicates, which is why Orai1 tests are treated as low-powered and an absent difference is reported as an absence of detected difference rather than as evidence of equivalence. Second, the calibration benchmark is used both to select the dominant variant of each toolchain and to report that variant\textquotesingle s performance, so the reported calibration figures are optimistic relative to a fully held-out evaluation, and the point is made again where the variants are selected.

\hypertarget{results}{%
\chapter{Results}\label{results}}

\hypertarget{calibration-dataset-1}{%
\section{Calibration Dataset}\label{calibration-dataset-1}}

Of the 308 source benchmark complexes, 303 were retained in the common calibration analysis for AutoDock Vina, DiffDock and EquiBind. Every generated pose was screened with the PoseBusters suite and compared with the crystal reference by symmetry-corrected heavy-atom RMSD and Kabsch RMSD. Pocket-guided EquiBind variants based on fpocket and P2Rank did not improve the variant-selection endpoint defined below relative to the unguided configuration.

Both learned pipelines were evaluated before and after local optimisation with smina and gnina. The toolchain variants were chosen on one three-way criterion, called the variant-selection endpoint throughout this thesis, under which a complex counts only when one of its poses is at once PoseBusters-valid, within 2\angstrom{} in place and within 1\angstrom{} in Kabsch form. It is deliberately stricter than the headline metric defined in the Methods chapter, which requires validity and the 2\angstrom{} in-place gate alone, and the two are never interchanged. Variant selection and the performance evaluation that follows were both carried out on this same calibration benchmark, with no held-out split between them, so the calibration figures reported below are optimistic relative to a fully independent evaluation. For DiffDock the two refiners are separated by at most two complexes on the variant-selection endpoint, at 76 against 74 complexes at rank-1 and 133 against 132 at the top-15 depth, so the choice between them is immaterial on this criterion, although gnina yields the higher pooled physical validity at 87.1\% against 84.5\%. For unguided EquiBind, gnina produced the clearly higher validity and combined recovery. These are empirical configuration results. The study did not measure optimisation trajectories and therefore does not establish that one refiner is inherently gentler or more aggressive.

Accordingly, the main comparison uses DiffDock + smina and EquiBind + gnina as the primary refined variants. Tables and the first mention in each analysis use the full pipeline names. Subsequent prose may shorten them to the generator names for readability, while raw or alternative variants are always qualified explicitly. Appendix analyses that use raw or gnina-optimised DiffDock output identify the variant explicitly. Their values should not be interchanged with the main comparison.

The AutoDock arm was selected on the same variant-selection endpoint, across the six variants of Table~\ref{tab:results-pose-production}. The scoring family is decided first and decisively. Vina reaches 71 complexes at rank-1 and 117 at the top-15 depth against 33 and 42 for the Vinardo reparametrisation on matched searches, an exact McNemar p of 2.8e-06 and 7.0e-18, so Vinardo is retained only as a scoring-function contrast, reported in Table~\ref{tab:results-pose-production} itself and otherwise described at protocol level in Appendix~\ref{docking-protocols}, while Vina supplies every AutoDock result discussed in the remainder of the main text. Within the Vina family the three treatments are far closer, at 65, 71 and 69 complexes at rank-1 and 112, 117 and 111 at top-15 for the raw search, gnina rescoring and gnina refinement respectively. As with DiffDock\textquotesingle s two refiners, no pair is separable on this endpoint, with gnina rescoring against the raw search at p = 0.405 and p = 0.063 and against gnina refinement at p = 0.774 and p = 0.109. The choice therefore rests on physical validity, where rescoring is never worse than the alternatives and gives the highest pooled rate at 99.5\% against 98.2\% for the raw search and 92.6\% for refinement, the last of these losing validity not because it moves atoms, since rescoring moves them too and gains validity, but because the learned score rather than the empirical function drives its final geometry. AutoDock Vina + gnina is accordingly the dominant AutoDock variant and is used throughout the main Results, matching DiffDock + smina and EquiBind + gnina in being a single named variant per family.

One asymmetry in what that rescoring does is worth stating, because it is easy to read the three refinement columns as equivalent interventions. Vina already performs a local optimisation inside its own search, so a post-hoc gnina pass adds a learned ranking to a pipeline that is already complete, which is why it moves AutoDock only from 25.7\% to 29.4\% at rank-1 on the validity-aware near-native endpoint. For DiffDock and EquiBind the same pass repairs geometry that the generator never optimised, which is why it transforms their validity. The ordering of the three pipelines is unchanged by the choice of AutoDock variant, since DiffDock + smina leads at every depth on either arm, but the size of that lead is not, so raw and rescored AutoDock numbers are never compared with each other in what follows.

