\hypertarget{posebusters-validity}{%
\subsection{PoseBusters Validity}\label{posebusters-validity}}

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.78\textheight,keepaspectratio]{media/media/image2.png}
\caption{Post-hoc optimisation and PoseBusters validity. Each tool is shown raw beside its optimised variants, with the dominant variant of each family being AutoDock Vina + gnina, DiffDock + smina and EquiBind + gnina. The panel token \texttt{gnina-opt} denotes the gnina rescoring arm called AutoDock Vina + gnina throughout this thesis, and \texttt{gnina CNN-refine} denotes the separate gnina refinement arm, which is not the reported pipeline. Significance brackets carry Holm-corrected p-values, where *** p \textless{} 0.001, ** p \textless{} 0.01 and * p \textless{} 0.05}\label{fig:results-r1}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:results-r1} shows per-complex PoseBusters-validity yield across the 303 benchmark complexes for raw and refined outputs, with each variant\textquotesingle s complex and pose counts matching Table~\ref{tab:results-pose-production}. Post-hoc minimisation is the dominant driver of validity for the learned tools. The DiffDock median rises from 13.3\% to 93.3\% with smina and 96.6\% with gnina, and unguided EquiBind rises from 0\% to 26.7\% with smina and 56.7\% with gnina. The gains are near-unanimous across complexes and remain statistically detectable after Holm correction, with matched-pairs rank-biserial effects of 0.994 to 1.000 and adjusted p-values below 10\textsuperscript{-35} throughout. The Hodges-Lehmann median gain for DiffDock is +62.1 percentage points with smina and +65.0 with gnina. Within-tool omnibus tests confirm the effect, at Friedman \ensuremath{\chi}\textsuperscript{2}(2) = 506.4 with Kendall\textquotesingle s W = 0.84 for DiffDock and \ensuremath{\chi}\textsuperscript{2}(2) = 416.2 with W = 0.69 for EquiBind. gnina is clearly stronger than smina for EquiBind, while the DiffDock refiners are much closer.

AutoDock behaves differently, and the three AutoDock boxes in the panel are what motivate the choice of its dominant variant. The raw search is already at the ceiling with a median per-complex yield of 100\%, a pooled validity of 98.2\% across all produced poses and at least one PoseBusters-valid pose for every one of the 303 complexes, that is full coverage with no ligand-preparation dropouts. Rescoring with gnina therefore leaves the median unchanged and instead lifts pooled validity to 99.5\%, a shift carried by the minority of complexes that still had invalid poses to recover, at Friedman \ensuremath{\chi}\textsuperscript{2}(2) = 322.8 with Kendall\textquotesingle s W = 0.53 over all 303 complexes and a rescoring-against-raw rank-biserial of 0.84 with p\_holm = 1.7e-07 over the 49 complexes whose valid-pose count differs between the two arms. Refinement moves the median the other way, down to 93.3\% for a Hodges-Lehmann loss of 5.0 percentage points, because minimising the coordinates of a pose that is already physically admissible has little left to repair and tends to move it off that admissible geometry, although this comparison does not isolate the mechanism. This is the empirical basis for taking rescoring rather than refinement as the dominant AutoDock treatment, and it is the mirror image of the learned tools, where the same minimisation is what makes their output usable at all. Physical validity is therefore where the physics-based engine retains its clearest advantage, and it is largely unaffected by removing the search box, because a pose minimised against an objective containing an explicit repulsion term is admissible wherever in the receptor it happens to sit.

Table~\ref{tab:results-pb-decomposition} decomposes those pass rates over the three check groups defined in the Methods. Almost none of the discrimination comes from chemistry or from covalent geometry. Not one of the 81,373 poses in the nine variants of the table fails any of the six chemical-validity checks, so in every variant the pose file and the receptor file parse, and the ligand passes sanitisation, converts to a standard InChI string, remains a single connected component and carries no radical atoms. Covalent geometry is almost as clean, with 25 bond-length failures and 22 bond-angle failures across those same 81,373 poses, that is 0.03\% on each check. Nearly all of the spread in Table~\ref{tab:results-pose-production} therefore sits in the intermolecular group, whose failure rate almost accounts for the invalid share on its own, at 75.2\% failing against a 75.6\% invalid share for raw DiffDock and 96.8\% against 97.1\% for raw EquiBind. Within that group a single check dominates, namely minimum distance to protein, which alone is failed by 73.8\% of raw DiffDock poses and 96.5\% of raw EquiBind poses against 0.2\% for AutoDock Vina + gnina. The intramolecular group contributes materially for one tool only, since raw EquiBind fails it on 27.9\% of its poses against at most 3.8\% for any of the AutoDock or DiffDock variants in the table.

The per-check sweep that the analysis plan declares as a Benjamini-Hochberg family confirms that pattern. Comparing the three dominant variants complex by complex across all 303 complexes with a Friedman test on per-complex failure rates, one check at a time, the six chemical-validity checks are constant at zero and so supply no hypothesis, which shrinks the family to the sixteen checks that vary. Thirteen of those sixteen survive correction at q \textless{} 0.05, and minimum distance to protein carries by far the strongest signal at \ensuremath{\chi}\textsuperscript{2}(2) = 393.3 with q = 6.2e-85, followed by volume overlap with protein at \ensuremath{\chi}\textsuperscript{2}(2) = 299.0 with q = 9.5e-65. Bond lengths and bond angles separate the variants but only trivially, at q = 8.3e-03 on a mean per-complex failure rate of 0.10\% for DiffDock + smina against zero for the other two variants. The three flatness checks do not survive, at q = 0.155 for aromatic ring flatness and q = 0.368 for both non-aromatic ring non-flatness and double-bond flatness.

Restricting the same decomposition to poses already within 2\angstrom{} of the crystal ligand separates validity from placement, and most of the apparent gap turns out to be placement. Intermolecular failure for DiffDock + smina falls from 14.6\% over all produced poses to 5.4\% over its 1,715 near-native poses, and for EquiBind + gnina from 47.1\% to 18.0\% over 373 near-native poses. The pass-all rates converge accordingly, to 94.6\% for DiffDock + smina and 81.8\% for EquiBind + gnina against 98.2\% for AutoDock Vina + gnina, so the 99.5\% against 51.8\% spread of Table~\ref{tab:results-pose-production} narrows to roughly sixteen percentage points once the pose is in the right place. A residual gap nevertheless survives that conditioning, because EquiBind + gnina still fails the intermolecular group on 67 of its 373 near-native poses against 4 of 221 for AutoDock Vina + gnina. Both of those subsets are small, so the residual difference is better read as a direction than as a calibrated effect size.

One qualification bears on how much independent corroboration this decomposition supplies. The group that does nearly all of the discriminating is also the group the search objective optimises against directly, because the Vina scoring function carries an explicit steric-repulsion term over ligand-receptor atom pairs, and minimum distance to protein together with volume overlap with protein are close to readings of that same term. A pose emitted by AutoDock, or minimised afterwards by smina or by gnina, has therefore already been driven down the coordinate that PoseBusters then scores it on, so part of the physics-based advantage is constitutive rather than an independent verdict. The refinement arm shows how tightly that coupling holds, and it does so by conceding the mechanism rather than by resisting it. gnina refinement is the one AutoDock treatment that replaces the empirical objective with the learned score, so it is the one arm whose final geometry is not driven by the explicit repulsion term, and it is also the only AutoDock Vina arm with a material intermolecular failure rate, at 5.4\% against 0.1\% for the raw search and 0.2\% for rescoring. The intermolecular verdict therefore tracks the presence of that specific term rather than the fact of having been minimised at all, which is the strong form of the objection and is accepted here.

Two observations nevertheless keep the wider finding from dissolving into the circularity. The first is that raw EquiBind carries an intramolecular defect that no scoring convention explains, with 27.9\% of its poses failing at least one intramolecular check, driven by internal steric clash at 22.1\% and gross conformational strain at 23.3\%, and it retains 10.7\% intramolecular failure even on its near-native poses. That axis is not a reading of any docking objective and it orders the tools the same way. The second is quantitative and is set out in the next paragraph, namely that the validity requirement barely moves the recovery endpoint on which the tool comparison rests, so however much of the validity advantage is constitutive, it is not what produces the reported ordering.

There is a third and more direct check on how far that circularity reaches, namely to ask what the validity requirement actually costs the headline endpoint. Because the endpoint is a conjunction, the question has an exact answer, obtained by counting the complexes recovered under the 2 \angstrom{} criterion alone and again under the same criterion with the PoseBusters requirement added. On the three variants carried through the Results the requirement removes at most four complexes of 303 at any depth. For AutoDock Vina + gnina it removes one complex at rank-1 and at top-5 and none at top-15 or top-30, for DiffDock + smina three to four complexes at every depth, and for unguided EquiBind + gnina one to three, which is a span of 0.00 to 1.32 percentage points across all three variants and all four depths. The recovery ordering, the paired differences and the intervals reported above are therefore set almost entirely by the 2 \angstrom{} placement criterion, and they would barely move if the validity requirement were dropped altogether. Whatever advantage the physics-based engine draws from being scored on a quantity its own objective contains, that advantage is not what produces the recovery numbers, because the gate those numbers pass through is nearly transparent for every variant this chapter reports.

The requirement bites hard in exactly one place, namely the raw learned output that this work recommends against deploying. For raw DiffDock it removes 47 complexes at rank-1 and 55 to 58 at the deeper pools, a span of 15.5 to 19.1 percentage points, so the same gate that costs a refined pipeline one complex costs the unrefined one nearly a fifth of the set. That contrast is the useful form of the validity result. Physical validity discriminates decisively between raw and minimised learned output, and only marginally between the minimised pipelines, which is why refinement rather than tool choice is where it changes a practical decision.

