\hypertarget{near-nativeness-and-form}{%
\subsection{Near-Nativeness and Form}\label{near-nativeness-and-form}}

Figures~\ref{fig:results-r2a} and \ref{fig:results-r2b} and Table~\ref{tab:results-near-native-form} summarise accuracy over the same 303 calibration complexes. The vertical axis reports the percentage of complexes for which a tool has at least one PoseBusters-valid pose within the distance threshold, evaluated over the best of its first d ranked poses.

Both figures are gated on PoseBusters validity and drawn at three ranking depths, with Figure~\ref{fig:results-r2a} measuring near-nativeness against as-placed crystal RMSD and Figure~\ref{fig:results-r2b} internal form against best-fit Kabsch RMSD.

Attention is drawn to the band between the top-1, top-15 and top-30 curves of each tool. Its width is the ranking headroom, that is the near-native valid poses that were generated but failed to rank first or within the top d, and it therefore separates sampling power from scoring power, a distinction the following paragraphs return to.

The three tools differ at every ranking depth on both metrics, with the Cochran\textquotesingle s Q omnibus tests significant throughout (near-nativeness p \textless{} 1e-7, form p \textless{} 1e-9). At the 2\angstrom{} in-place RMSD threshold DiffDock has the highest point estimate at every depth, with rank-1 accuracies of 34.0\% for DiffDock, 29.4\% for AutoDock Vina + gnina and 15.2\% for EquiBind. That lead is not statistically separable from AutoDock, however, since Holm-corrected exact McNemar tests leave the DiffDock advantage over the gnina-rescored arm short of significance at every depth (p\_holm = 0.219 at rank-1, 0.188 at top-15 and 0.136 at top-30), while both tools remain decisively ahead of EquiBind throughout (all p\_holm \ensuremath{\leq} 3e-5). Because a non-significant test is not evidence of equivalence, the contrast between AutoDock Vina + gnina and DiffDock + smina is reported as a paired difference with its interval rather than as a failed test. Over the 303 paired complexes the DiffDock advantage on the valid-near-native endpoint is +4.6 percentage points at rank-1, with a 95\% confidence interval of \ensuremath{-}2.2 to +11.4 points, +2.6 points at top-5 (\ensuremath{-}4.6 to +9.8), +5.3 points at top-15 (\ensuremath{-}2.1 to +12.5) and +5.9 points at top-30 (\ensuremath{-}1.4 to +13.2). Each is a Newcombe hybrid score interval for the difference of correlated proportions, taken over the 303 complex-level pairs and computed by \texttt{newcombe\_paired\_diff\_ci} in the shared statistics module. Every interval contains zero, but the upper bounds run from +9.8 to +13.2 points while the lower bounds reach only \ensuremath{-}4.6 to \ensuremath{-}1.4 points, so a DiffDock advantage of practical size stays admissible at every depth whereas an advantage of the same magnitude for AutoDock Vina does not. The evidence is therefore inconclusive about the ordering of these two pipelines on near-nativeness. How much that leaves unresolved is itself quantifiable, and the paragraph below bounds it rather than leaving the contrast as a failed test.

That imprecision is a property of the design rather than a shortfall in execution, and it can be stated exactly. A paired binary test carries information only from the complexes on which the two pipelines disagree, because a complex both recover, or neither recovers, says nothing about which is better. At rank-1 both recover 40 of the 303 and neither recovers 151, so only the remaining 112 discordant complexes, 37.0\% of the set, resolve the contrast. That discordant count rather than the 303 sets the resolution. It puts the minimum detectable difference at 9.8 percentage points for 80\% power and leaves the observed 4.6-point difference tested at a power of 0.26, so a difference of the size actually present was unlikely to reach significance whatever the pipelines did. Detecting it reliably would require roughly 1,400 paired complexes, and even the 428 entries of the earlier preprint release of this benchmark would leave the minimum detectable difference near 8.2 points, so the ambiguity cannot be resolved by enlarging this benchmark. Two one-sided tests bound the same contrast from the other side. At a margin of 12 percentage points the two pipelines are equivalent at rank-1, at top-5 and at top-15, and at top-30 equivalence requires a slightly wider margin of 12.1 points, whereas no depth is equivalent within 5 points and only top-5 is within 10. The defensible statement is therefore that the data exclude a difference larger than about twelve percentage points in either direction while remaining unable to resolve one smaller than that, which is a bounded claim rather than an absence of one. On form at 1\angstrom{} the same ordering holds, at rank-1 accuracies of 52.1\% for DiffDock, 46.9\% for AutoDock Vina + gnina and 30.7\% for EquiBind, and here the DiffDock advantage over AutoDock does reach significance at the top-15 depth (p\_holm = 0.023) though not at rank-1 or top-30 (p\_holm = 0.109 and 0.161). The defensible reading is therefore that DiffDock is ahead of the classical engine on point estimates at every depth and on both metrics, but that the two are separable only on form at intermediate depth, whereas the gap of both to EquiBind is unambiguous.

This point-estimate ordering is the reverse of the one obtained when AutoDock Vina was given a crystal-ligand-centred box, and the reversal motivates the matched blind comparison. Under blind conditions the diffusion model has higher in-place accuracy point estimates, without a statistically established advantage over AutoDock Vina, while the physics-based search retains a decisive advantage in physical validity, at a pooled 99.5\% for AutoDock Vina + gnina against 84.5\% for DiffDock + smina. Accuracy and admissibility are therefore separable properties, and the tool with the higher point estimate on one need not lead on the other.

The EquiBind deficit remains greater on in-place accuracy than on conformational form. Within each tool a Cochran's Q across the three depths confirms that ranking depth changes the outcome (Vina Q = 124, DiffDock Q = 128, EquiBind Q = 30, all p \textless{} 1e-6). The recovered near-native share from top-1 to top-15, with Wilson 95\% intervals, is +20.5\% (16.3 to 25.4) for AutoDock Vina + gnina, +21.1\% (16.9 to 26.1) for DiffDock + smina and only +5.0\% (3.0 to 8.0) for EquiBind + gnina. The further step from top-15 to top-30 adds at most 0.7 percentage points for any of the three reported variants, two further complexes for DiffDock + smina, so almost all recoverable headroom is already present in a top-15 pool.

