The band between the curves quantifies that separation \cite{ref050}, \cite{ref052}, since it shows how often a good pose is generated but not ranked first.

AutoDock Vina no longer has the highest point estimate on either near-nativeness or form once the crystal-centred box is withdrawn, and its ranking headroom remains substantial. Rank-1 valid-near-native recovery of 29.4\% rises to 49.8\% when the best of the top-15 poses is selected retrospectively, a gain of 62 complexes that is close to DiffDock\textquotesingle s 64. Rescoring with gnina recovers part of that headroom prospectively rather than removing it, since the same figures for the unoptimised search are 25.7\% and 47.9\% with a gain of 67, so the learned rescorer converts a handful of retrospectively available poses into prospectively selected ones without closing the selection gap. The stronger conclusion is therefore that under a blind search the classical engine is no better at ranking its own output than the diffusion model is at ranking its own, and that both leave a large selection gap, consistent with the longstanding distinction between sampling and scoring \cite{ref003}, \cite{ref050}, \cite{ref052}.

DiffDock\textquotesingle s rank-1 pose is near-native in 34.0\% and form-correct in 52.1\% of the calibration set, ahead of AutoDock Vina on both point estimates. Its top-30 pool contains a valid near-native pose for 55.8\% of complexes and a form-correct pose for 74.6\% at the primary 1 \angstrom{} Kabsch threshold. Although DiffDock provides a confidence model \cite{ref024}, independent evaluations likewise show that generative docking can sample acceptable poses more reliably than it ranks them \cite{ref013}, \cite{ref032}, \cite{ref035}.

