\end{figure}

The depth-resolved curves separate generation from selection. DiffDock has the highest point estimate at every depth, and both it and AutoDock Vina retain material ranking headroom of comparable size. Both therefore carry a material selection component of comparable absolute size, 62 complexes for AutoDock Vina + gnina and 64 for DiffDock + smina, because valid near-native poses often appear below rank-1. That component is not the larger part of their residual loss. About seven in ten of their rank-1 failures hold no qualifying pose anywhere in the pool, so generation and localisation remain the greater constraint for both. EquiBind is limited almost entirely at generation and localisation because its best-of-pool recovery remains low \cite{ref003}, \cite{ref013}, \cite{ref024}, \cite{ref035}. A top-15 pool captures almost all of the observed near-nativeness headroom, since extending the pool to 30 adds at most 0.7 additional percentage points. The form endpoint still moves on that extension, gaining 5.0 points for AutoDock Vina + gnina, from 66.0 to 71.0\%, 2.3 points for DiffDock + smina, from 72.3 to 74.6\%, and 3.6 points for EquiBind + gnina, from 46.2 to 49.8\%, at the primary 1 \angstrom{} Kabsch threshold of Table~\ref{tab:results-near-native-form}.

Figure~\ref{fig:results-r3} separates the two ways a valid pose can be wrong, plotting in-place RMSD against best-fit Kabsch RMSD so that placement-limited poses (correctly shaped but mislocated), form-limited poses (placed roughly right but folded wrong) and mixed cases fall in distinct regions, with the three columns widening the accepted pool from rank-1 to the best of the top-5 and top-15 and the arrow tracing each cohort median\textquotesingle s drift from rank-1. Table~\ref{tab:results-placement-form} gives the underlying distributions.\footnote{Poses whose docked centroid lies more than 8\angstrom{} from the crystal-ligand site are treated as outside the crystal-site neighbourhood and excluded before plotting, which removes 5,907 poses at the top-15 depth and leaves 5,549 PoseBusters-valid poses within that neighbourhood. The trim therefore discards a little over half of the 11,456 plotted candidates, so the panel shows the near-site poses only and is not a picture of the full output. Excluded poses are not off the receptor, since almost all of them remain in van der Waals contact with the protein, and they are set aside only because they sit away from the crystal site. Both axes are then clipped at 5\angstrom{} to keep the dense near-native core legible, so a further 2,188 of those 5,549 poses lie beyond the frame and are not drawn. This is consistent with the in-place third quartiles reported in Table~\ref{tab:results-placement-form}, which exceed 5\angstrom{} at every depth for EquiBind and from the top-5 pool onwards for AutoDock Vina, while DiffDock stays below that mark throughout.}

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.78\textheight,keepaspectratio]{media/media/image5.png}
\caption{Form versus in-place RMSD across ranking depth. Rows are the dominant variant of each family, namely AutoDock Vina + gnina, DiffDock + smina and EquiBind + gnina}\label{fig:results-r3}
\end{figure}

AutoDock Vina + gnina returns a PoseBusters-valid pose for all 303 complexes, although only 58.4\% of them still contribute once the cohort is trimmed to the crystal-site neighbourhood at rank-1, which is the coverage column of Table~\ref{tab:results-placement-form}. Yet increasing ranking depth causes a decline in in-place accuracy. Its median in-place RMSD drifts from 1.9\angstrom{} at rank-1 to 5.6\angstrom{} at top-15, the largest movement of any tool, and a receptor-bootstrap interval on that +3.69\angstrom{} drift spans +2.75 to +4.26\angstrom{}, so it is comfortably separated from zero even though the two cohorts do not cover the same complexes. A separate within-complex test across disjoint rank bands also detects a monotone trend in placement on the 130 complexes that populate all three bands (Friedman p \ensuremath{\approx} 6e-15). Meanwhile its placement-limited share swells from 51\% to 81\% and its form-limited share shrinks from 19\% to 5\%. Increasing ranking depth mostly adds correctly-shaped ligands scattered in the wrong place, consistent with the highest pose diversity of the three at a 5.7\angstrom{} within-complex spread, so its ranking is informative only near the very top.

DiffDock is the depth-stable tool. Its rank-1 coverage is lower, but its cloud moves least, with an in-place drift of +0.46\angstrom{} from 1.467 to 1.929\angstrom{} against +3.69\angstrom{} for AutoDock Vina and +1.21\angstrom{} for EquiBind. Widening the pool fifteen-fold leaves the median near 1.5 to 1.9\angstrom{} in in-place RMSD and about 0.9\angstrom{} in form, from 0.885 to 0.918\angstrom{}, and by top-5 and top-15 it is significantly the best tool on both axes. The form error moves by only +0.03\angstrom{} across that fifteen-fold widening, and a receptor-bootstrap interval on that drift spans \ensuremath{-}0.05 to +0.12\angstrom{}, so it cannot be distinguished from zero. A separate within-complex test across disjoint rank bands does detect a monotone trend in form on the 137 complexes that populate all three bands (Friedman p = 0.006), so the ordering is real while the magnitude is negligible. Part of that stability is low pose diversity, namely a 2.34\angstrom{} within-complex spread of near-duplicate poses against 5.74\angstrom{} for AutoDock Vina, so its robustness to depth is partly a reluctance to diversify.

EquiBind can generate PoseBusters-valid output, but validity does not translate into reliable in-place accuracy. At least one valid pose occurs somewhere in the full output for 247 of 303 complexes, yet valid-near-native recovery remains only 20.1\% by the top-15 depth and 20.5\% over the full thirty-pose pool. Its rank-1 median in-place RMSD of 2.9 \angstrom{} already exceeds the 2 \angstrom{} near-native threshold. gnina refinement can remove some local geometric defects, but it does not correct the pipeline\textquotesingle s gross localisation failures.

Deeper ranks therefore add valid but mislocated poses for every tool, steeply for AutoDock and EquiBind and barely for DiffDock, so in-place RMSD rather than internal form is the axis that degrades with depth. DiffDock\textquotesingle s more form-limited colouring is an artefact of its low in-place RMSD inflating the form-to-in-place ratio, since its absolute form RMSD is the lowest of the three.

