Figure~\ref{fig:results-r5} (A) shows that DiffDock now contributes most to the crystal cluster, with a median of six of its fifteen ranked poses inside it and 82\% reach, ahead of AutoDock at a median of three poses and 76\% reach, while EquiBind remains all-or-nothing at a median of zero and 49\% reach. The ordering is significant on both pose count (Friedman \ensuremath{\chi}\textsuperscript{2} = 53.8, Kendall W = 0.089) and reach (Cochran's Q = 107.4), and once the 4 \angstrom{} gate is applied the two measures agree on which pairs are separable. On pose count DiffDock is clear of both rivals, at rank-biserial 0.49 against AutoDock and 0.37 against EquiBind with Holm-corrected Wilcoxon p \textless{} 1e-4 in both cases, and AutoDock is now also separable from EquiBind at 0.16 (Holm p = 0.032), while on reach every pair separates as well, the AutoDock and DiffDock difference reaching Holm-corrected McNemar p = 0.040 and both tools remaining decisively ahead of EquiBind. As evident in Figure~\ref{fig:results-r5} (B) the correct pocket is usually a multi-tool basin, yet the consensus is polarised, namely complexes tend to be solved by all three tools at once (118 against 90.9 expected, permutation p \textless{} 1e-4) or by none of them (24 against 6.9 expected), with the intermediate states under-represented at 52 single-tool complexes against 59.1 expected and 109 two-tool complexes against 146.2. Where one tool does solve a complex alone it is usually DiffDock, in 31 of those 52 cases against 19 for AutoDock. Internal tightness in Figure~\ref{fig:results-r5} (C) no longer inverts the reach order, since DiffDock is now both the tool that reaches the crystal cluster most often and the one whose own poses sit tightest at a median 0.37 \angstrom{} over the 232 complexes on which its spread is defined. What survives is the AutoDock anomaly. Its poses remain the loosest at 0.78 \angstrom{} over 204 complexes, behind EquiBind at 0.59 \angstrom{} over 138 (Kruskal--Wallis H = 54.4, p \textless{} 1e-4, \ensuremath{\varepsilon}\textsuperscript{2} = 0.092). Those three groups are not the same set of complexes, because a tool contributes a spread only where it places at least two poses in the crystal cluster, so the missingness is informative rather than random and an unpaired test of them is only indicative. Restricting the comparison to the 102 complexes on which all three tools are measurable and treating them as paired observations still separates the three (Friedman \ensuremath{\chi}\textsuperscript{2} = 34.8, p \textless{} 1e-4), so the difference is not an artefact of the differing composition. When AutoDock does find the pocket it therefore scatters its poses across it rather than converging on one placement.

Figure~\ref{fig:results-r5} (D) shows cluster radius rising from a median 1.97 \angstrom{} for single-tool clusters to 3.52 \angstrom{} at two tools and 3.86 \angstrom{} at all three (Jonckheere--Terpstra z = 6.75, p \textless{} 1e-4), but the trend disappears after controlling for pose count (partial \ensuremath{\rho} = \ensuremath{-}0.04, p = 0.47). The partition is reproducible under resampling (median bootstrap Jaccard 0.78). Internal indices are interpreted only for eligible multi-cluster complexes. Under the separate 4 \angstrom{} crystal-site threshold, the retrospective oracle finds a recovered cluster in 93\% of complexes, whereas the best inexpensive first-cluster rule reaches 55\%. Consensus ranking is the strongest of the five prospective rules, tied with its medoid-centred variant at 55\%, and it beats the legacy pose-count rule at 49\% (Holm-corrected McNemar p = 0.010, omnibus Cochran's Q = 171.4). The 38-point shortfall against the oracle is nearly three times the 13-point shortfall seen under the superseded boxed search. This gap is a site-ranking problem. The crystal-compatible region is usually represented somewhere in the pooled pose cloud, but selecting it prospectively becomes markedly harder once the search is blind. Figure~\ref{fig:results-r6} summarises the internal validity indices, the resampling stability and the precision-at-one of all five ranking rules against that oracle ceiling.

In this benchmark, binding-site agreement is driven by DiffDock and AutoDock with EquiBind as the consensus bottleneck. The strongest redundancy is the one between the two learned tools at rank-1 and top-5, although by top-15 all three pairings have converged and none of them is strongest. The clustering is geometrically sound and the crystal-compatible region is present in the pooled ensemble for 93\% of complexes, so for the pooled ensemble the residual loss is in ranking rather than search, even though EquiBind\textquotesingle s own shortfall remains one of sampling. Pooling helps on both axes once the comparison is made like for like at the level of individual poses, taken over the full pose cloud rather than the top-15 pool the reach figures use. The best pose from the pooled three-tool ensemble lands within the 4 \angstrom{} crystal-site threshold for 95\% of complexes at a median oracle distance of 0.35 \angstrom{}, against 84\% and 0.56 \angstrom{} for DiffDock alone and 78\% and 0.73 \angstrom{} for AutoDock alone. Almost all of that gain comes from the two stronger arms, since AutoDock and DiffDock together already reach 94.7\% at 0.40 \angstrom{} and adding EquiBind moves reach by 0.3 percentage points. The pooled cluster centre is necessarily coarser than any single pose, at a median 1.30 \angstrom{}, which is a consequence of averaging over cluster members rather than a cost of pooling.

