\hypertarget{interaction-and-contact-analysis}{%
\subsection{Interaction and Contact Analysis}\label{interaction-and-contact-analysis}}

The purpose of docking is to reproduce the protein--ligand interactions that drive binding. The interactions of the crystal ligand poses are compared with the interactions of PoseBusters-valid poses produced by the toolchains, with typed contacts detected by the PandaMap implementation \cite{ref090} of the PLIP interaction-profiling rules \cite{ref054}, applied as heavy-atom distance and atom-type assignments, since the angular criteria are not evaluated on the code path used. This subsection was regenerated for the whole-protein search, so its AutoDock arm is AutoDock Vina + gnina throughout and is directly comparable with Table~\ref{tab:results-pose-production} and with the recovery analysis above. Because a typed fingerprint is keyed on residue identity, every pose and the crystal reference were profiled against one common author-numbered receptor per complex, verified heavy-atom coordinate-compatible with the receptor each pipeline actually docked into.\footnote{The prepared receptors the toolchains emit do not share a residue-numbering scheme. The MGLTools receptor used by the whole-protein AutoDock search renumbers residues sequentially from one, so its contacts cannot be matched against a crystal reference carrying author numbering. Profiling every arm against the common cleaned receptor relabels residues without moving any atom. That EquiBind, whose pipeline is untouched by the migration, returns a crystal Jaccard of 0.37 and an F1 of 0.44 both here and on the superseded boxed run is the check that the two runs remain on one scale.} Figure~\ref{fig:results-r7} reports the mean Jaccard overlap between typed interaction fingerprints, where each tool\textquotesingle s repertoire for a complex is the union of its top-5 ranked poses and the overlap is averaged over all shared complexes.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.78\textheight,keepaspectratio]{media/media/image9.png}
\caption{Mean Jaccard similarity of interaction fingerprints, whole-protein search}\label{fig:results-r7}
\end{figure}

Under the blind whole-protein search DiffDock + smina has the highest mean typed-interaction Jaccard similarity to the crystal fingerprint (0.45), followed by EquiBind + gnina (0.37) and AutoDock Vina + gnina (0.36). The ordering is the reverse of the superseded crystal-ligand-boxed run, on which the site-restricted AutoDock search reached 0.67 against 0.44 for DiffDock and 0.37 for EquiBind. The whole-protein protocol therefore costs AutoDock roughly half of its measured fingerprint overlap, 0.67 to 0.36, while the two blind pipelines are essentially unmoved, EquiBind + gnina returning 0.37 in both runs and DiffDock + smina shifting only from 0.44 to 0.45, which locates the change in the AutoDock protocol rather than in the contact detection. That contrast does not isolate the search box itself, because the boxed AutoDock arm was raw AutoDock Vina while the blind one is AutoDock Vina + gnina, and receptor preparation also differs between the runs. Pairwise tool similarities are lower, at 0.34 to 0.42, showing that the methods often make different contact hypotheses. Because Jaccard combines shared and non-shared interactions, these values should not be interpreted as contact recall. They show overall fingerprint agreement, not the fraction of native interactions recovered.

Figure~\ref{fig:results-r8} resolves precision, recall and F1 against pose rank k, where k is each tool\textquotesingle s own rank over all of its produced poses and only PoseBusters-valid poses are profiled. A complex therefore contributes no k = 1 point when its own top-ranked pose fails PoseBusters, which happens for 5 of the 303 complexes under AutoDock Vina + gnina and for 18 of the 300 complexes that retain a valid DiffDock + smina pose, whereas EquiBind\textquotesingle s gnina-affinity rank is assigned over its retained valid poses and so always starts at k = 1.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.78\textheight,keepaspectratio]{media/media/image10.png}
\caption{Native-interaction recovery versus the crystal by pose rank (1--5), whole-protein search}\label{fig:results-r8}
\end{figure}

At rank-1 the three pipelines are close, at an F1 of 0.53 for DiffDock + smina, 0.53 for AutoDock Vina + gnina and 0.46 for EquiBind + gnina, so on its own top-ranked pose the classical engine recovers the native contact pattern about as faithfully as the diffusion model. Those three means rest on different available-case cohorts, namely the 282, 298 and 247 complexes for which each pipeline has a PoseBusters-valid rank-1 pose to score, and no equivalence or non-inferiority test was applied to them. On the 278 complexes AutoDock Vina + gnina and DiffDock + smina share, the means are 0.5256 and 0.5266 with no detected paired difference (Wilcoxon p = 0.69). A sensitivity analysis that instead scores a missing valid rank-1 pose as zero across all 303 complexes reverses the order, at 0.517 for AutoDock Vina + gnina against 0.492 for DiffDock + smina, because DiffDock more often has no valid pose to nominate. The rank-1 comparison is therefore conditional on producing a valid top pose, and the ordering it gives is not robust to how that failure is counted. They separate with depth rather than at the top. AutoDock decays steadily to 0.36 by rank 5 while DiffDock stays near flat at 0.51 and EquiBind at 0.43, so DiffDock leads on the top-5 union of Figure~\ref{fig:results-r7} without leading on the pose a user would actually take. A per-complex Kendall \ensuremath{\tau} between rank and score, tested with a one-sample Wilcoxon signed-rank against zero and BH-corrected across the nine tool-by-metric cells, shows why. AutoDock\textquotesingle s own ranking puts better poses first (\ensuremath{\tau} = \ensuremath{-}0.20, \ensuremath{-}0.32 and \ensuremath{-}0.20 for precision, recall and F1, all BH q \textless{} 0.001), so its gnina re-ranking is informative about which of its poses to take, although only that arm was profiled here, so the effect of the re-ranking itself is not measured. EquiBind\textquotesingle s gnina-affinity rank works for recall and F1 (\ensuremath{\tau} = \ensuremath{-}0.26 and \ensuremath{-}0.20, both significant) and only marginally for precision (q = 0.015). DiffDock\textquotesingle s native confidence shows no detected association with interaction recovery (\ensuremath{\tau} \ensuremath{\approx} 0 on all three, none significant), so nothing in these data lets its rank-1 pose be expected to recover more native contacts than its rank-5. That is an absence of detected ordering rather than a demonstration that the confidence carries no signal. That this rank-ordering pattern is reproduced unchanged from the boxed run, on an AutoDock arm whose absolute level has halved, indicates it is a property of the ranking signals rather than of the search box.

Figure~\ref{fig:results-r9} decomposes each pose\textquotesingle s typed contacts into matched native contacts recovered, missed native contacts absent, and spurious non-native contacts invented.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.78\textheight,keepaspectratio]{media/media/image11.png}
\caption{Matched, missed and spurious typed contacts versus the crystal, by pose rank, whole-protein search}\label{fig:results-r9}
\end{figure}

At rank 1 AutoDock Vina + gnina and DiffDock + smina are almost indistinguishable, recovering 19.2 and 19.4 native contacts against 17.1 and 16.4 missed, with AutoDock inventing slightly more spurious contacts (15.7 against 14.1). The two diverge with depth. AutoDock falls to 12.8 matched against 23.4 missed by rank 5 and its spurious count climbs to 18.2, so it crosses from matched-dominated to missed-dominated within its own top five and is the only pipeline whose invented contacts increase significantly with rank, whereas DiffDock holds near 18.6 matched against 17.4 missed with a flat spurious count. EquiBind misses more native contacts than it recovers at every rank, 15.7 matched against 19.4 missed at rank 1, so it systematically under-reproduces the native network, though it invents the fewest non-native contacts (10.3 to 11.4). The same rank test orders AutoDock cleanly across all three classes (\ensuremath{\tau} = \ensuremath{-}0.32 matched, +0.32 missed, +0.11 spurious, all significant) and EquiBind on matched and missed (\ensuremath{\pm}0.26), whereas DiffDock\textquotesingle s counts are flat.

Taken together, the whole-protein interaction analysis mirrors the placement result. AutoDock Vina + gnina and DiffDock + smina show no detected difference in native interaction pattern on the pose each tool actually nominates, so neither can be preferred on rank-1 contact fidelity, although the data are inconclusive rather than positively equivalent. They differ in what lies beneath that pose. AutoDock\textquotesingle s series is steeply ordered, so its gnina ranking is informative but its lower-ranked poses are genuinely worse, while DiffDock\textquotesingle s series is flat and unordered, so its confidence wastes a pool whose depth is uniformly good and a re-scorer stands to help it most. EquiBind is limited at source, recovering few native contacts regardless of rank. The earlier reading, on which AutoDock reproduced the native pattern most faithfully by a wide margin, does not survive the whole-protein protocol, although that contrast does not isolate the search box because the boxed AutoDock arm was raw AutoDock Vina while the blind one adds a gnina pass.

