AutoDock Vina produces the highest experimental physical validity but not the highest usable yield. Of its 120 generated poses 119 pass PoseBusters, and 50 to 60\% per ligand also satisfy the placement rule, namely that a pose lies on the receptor and outside the transmembrane exclusion slab, giving 50\% for 2abp-NH2, 60\% for Synta-66 and 55\% for GSK-7975A. That near-perfect validity is empirical rather than guaranteed by classical docking, as the calibration analysis still contains invalid Vina poses. In the Orai1 panel, the 53 excluded Vina poses fail the operational slab criterion.

DiffDock + smina is noisier on validity but places better. Validity runs from 87.5\% to 100\%, so the minimisation delivers mostly sane geometry, and 60\% to 91.4\% of the valid poses lie outside the transmembrane exclusion slab, ahead of AutoDock on all three ligands and most widely for GSK-7975A at 91.4\% against 55\%. On the re-docked pose set every complex delivers its full pose budget, including the boron-containing 2-APB (2abp-NH2), whose 40 of 40 poses are all physically valid but leak most at the placement stage, with only 60\% of them outside the slab.

EquiBind performs poorly on both filters. Validity is only 17.5\% to 35\%, and the reason is a preparation step rather than a property of EquiBind itself. This panel is the one arm in the thesis that applied the in-protein UFF relaxation recorded in Table~\ref{tab:appendix-orai-protocols}, and that step fixes every protein atom before minimising, so on a buried pose it cannot slide the ligand out of the clash and relieves the overlap by deforming the ligand instead. The resulting bond-length and bond-angle failures are then permanent, because gnina relieves clashes rather than correcting covalent geometry. That the cause is the relaxation and not the generator is visible in the calibration benchmark, which ran the same tool without that step and returns 25 bond-length and 22 bond-angle failures across all 81,373 poses of its nine variants, EquiBind included, as reported with the decomposition above. The intramolecular column of Table~\ref{tab:results-pb-decomposition} is not a covalent-geometry column, and the 27.9\% it records for raw EquiBind is internal clash and conformational strain rather than distorted bonds. The Orai1 experimental EquiBind yields below should therefore be read as a lower bound on what this configuration can do, and the control panel, which did not apply the relaxation, is the fairer picture of the tool. The few valid poses it does produce also sit almost entirely inside the transmembrane exclusion slab. Of its 29 valid poses across the three ligands, only one survives the placement rule, so the usable yield is zero for Synta-66 and GSK-7975A and 2.5\% for 2-APB. For this target EquiBind contributes essentially nothing usable.

The funnel shows that physical validity alone overstates utility on this target. AutoDock Vina and DiffDock both perform strongly on PoseBusters validity, but the combined screen for on-receptor placement outside the transmembrane exclusion slab separates them from EquiBind. The final usable column is therefore the relevant operational endpoint for this study. AutoDock Vina yields 50 to 60\% usable poses, DiffDock is ahead of it on all three ligands at 60 to 80\%, and EquiBind retains only one pose. Because GSK-7975A and Synta-66 act at or near the pore and selectivity filter, the exclusion slab is a modelling choice rather than proof that every excluded pose is biologically wrong \cite{ref070}, \cite{ref071}.

\hypertarget{orai-channel-md-simulation}{%
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Orai Channel (MD Simulation)}{Orai Channel (MD Simulation)}}\label{orai-channel-md-simulation}}

The experimental target is the human Orai1 channel, the pore-forming subunit of the calcium release-activated calcium channel. It is a homohexamer arranged around a central ion-conduction pore \cite{ref037}. The model spans residues 66 to 288 of each subunit and includes the four transmembrane helices and flanking regions, giving 1,338 residues and 10,362 heavy atoms without ligand, Ca2+ or other heteroatoms in the delivered model. Those figures describe the model as delivered and as used for validity screening. The AutoDock search receptor for the Fr0 snapshot alone was prepared with a permissive residue filter that dropped five residues carrying 41 heavy atoms, so that one search ran against 1,333 residues and 10,321 heavy atoms, as recorded in Appendix~\ref{orai1-receptor-model}. Signature residues R91, V102 and the E106 selectivity filter are represented. Two of the three modulators, GSK-7975A and Synta-66, are reported to act at or near the extracellular pore mouth and the selectivity filter rather than inside the conduction pathway, whereas the site of 2-APB is not experimentally resolved, and the mechanisms and preferred regions of the three are in any case not identical \cite{ref040,ref041}, \cite{ref070}, \cite{ref071}.

The receptor ensemble contains four molecular-dynamics snapshots of the same channel, namely an equilibrated starting structure (Fr0) and three later frames (Fr300, Fr400 and Fr499). The files follow a membrane-simulation preparation consistent with the structural and molecular-dynamics work of the contributing Orai group \cite{ref038}, \cite{ref072}. Docking against all four frames provides limited ensemble sampling and partly mitigates, but does not remove, the rigid-receptor approximation \cite{ref036}.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.78\textheight,keepaspectratio]{media/media/image12.png}
\caption[The docking receptor]{The docking receptor. Cartoon representation of the Fr300 molecular-dynamics snapshot (Orai1WT-MDSnap-Fr300), drawn in an oblique view. The image is a one-off illustrative render prepared in PyMOL for this figure and is not the output of any analysis script, so it carries no measurement and none of the quantities in the surrounding text is read from it}\label{fig:results-r10}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:results-r10} shows the receptor used throughout this chapter, the Fr300 snapshot. The view is oblique and the six subunits are not colour-coded, so the hexameric arrangement around the central conduction pore is a property of the model described above rather than something the render itself resolves. The four frames are not evenly distributed. The starting structure lies approximately 7.3 to 7.5 \angstrom{} from the three tightly clustered later snapshots before superposition, while the overall pore architecture remains similar. The same picture holds after alignment, since a Kabsch superposition on the 1,338 C\ensuremath{\alpha} atoms still leaves the starting frame 5.90, 5.88 and 6.02 \angstrom{} from Fr300, Fr400 and Fr499, whereas those three lie within 0.93 to 1.19 \angstrom{} of one another, so the reading of one outlier frame against a tight trio rests on an aligned number rather than on rigid-body offset alone. Orai is also atypical relative to the largely soluble calibration benchmark because its top-ranked cavity is the elongated, hydrated ion pore rather than a conventional buried hydrophobic pocket.

Crucially, the ensemble carries no crystallographic reference, so there is no RMSD-to-native ground truth for the Orai complexes, and the crystal-dependent analyses used for the calibration benchmark, namely near-nativeness, the form-versus-placement decomposition and native-interaction recovery, do not carry over. Operational admissibility is instead read from the ensemble itself, namely the placement of poses relative to the transmembrane exclusion slab, cross-tool and cross-frame clustering consensus, physical validity and the consistency of predicted interaction contacts, each reported in the subsections that follow. These are hypothesis-compatibility proxies rather than measures of accuracy, since nothing in the Orai1 arm can be scored against an experimental pose. A fuller account of the receptor model, its structural composition, molecular-dynamics provenance and pocket-detection profile, is given in the Appendix (Orai1 Receptor Model).

\hypertarget{posebusters-validity-1}{%
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{PoseBusters Validity}{ PoseBusters Validity}}\label{posebusters-validity-1}}

On Orai1, physical validity is the per-unit percentage of retained poses that pass all PoseBusters checks (Table~\ref{tab:appendix-posebusters-checks}). The denominator is every pose a unit contributes to the analysis after the top-ten cap, and no quality filter is applied within it, so nothing is removed for being invalid, off-receptor or inside the exclusion slab. The cap itself is a selection rather than a neutral truncation on the experimental panel, where thirty poses were written per unit and the best ten by each tool\textquotesingle s own ranking are kept, that ranking being the gnina CNNaffinity re-ranking on the AutoDock arm, against ten written and ten kept on the control panel. The experimental panel therefore enters the cross-panel comparison with the more heavily selected pose set, which is recorded with the other panel differences in Table~\ref{tab:appendix-orai-protocols}. This endpoint measures chemical and geometric admissibility, not correct placement. The separate operational placement screen is applied in the next subsection.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.78\textheight,keepaspectratio]{media/media/image13.png}
\caption{PoseBusters-valid yield of produced poses on Orai, benchmark against experimental ligands}\label{fig:results-r11}
\end{figure}

In Figure~\ref{fig:results-r11} AutoDock Vina is the most stable of the three across the two ligand sets. Its median yield is 100\% on both, and on the twelve experimental frame-ligand units it reaches a mean of 99.2\% with an interquartile range pinned at 100\%, against a benchmark mean of 91.6\% with a thin failure tail in which 2\% of units yield nothing. DiffDock is the tool that moves most between the sets, its benchmark median of 80\% and mean of 78.5\% rising to an experimental median of 100\% and mean of 93.3\% with an interquartile range of 88 to 100\%. EquiBind is the weakest tool in both sets, its experimental median falling to 5.0\% and its mean to 24.2\%, with an interquartile range running from 0 to 53\%.

The ceiling and floor rates separate the tools more sharply than the medians do. On the benchmark AutoDock returns a fully clean unit, in which every produced pose is valid, for 77.1\% of units against 22.2\% for DiffDock, a 3.5-fold gap, since on that panel the middle half of DiffDock units falls between 70 and 90\% and rarely reaches a perfect unit. AutoDock and DiffDock are much closer on the experimental ligands, at 91.7\% against 66.7\%, so DiffDock is fully clean in two thirds of the experimental units. AutoDock and EquiBind are skewed in opposite directions, AutoDock being ceiling-bound with a mean below its median while EquiBind is floor-bound with a benchmark mean of 42.4\% above its median of 30\%, so no single central value describes both. EquiBind is best read as all-or-nothing rather than mediocre, its lower benchmark quartile pinned at zero with 27\% of units yielding nothing against 16\% fully clean, and on the experimental ligands both tails move the wrong way, 50\% of units yielding nothing and not one reaching a fully clean result, its best unit stopping at 70\%.

Per tool the sets were compared with an unpaired two-sided Mann-Whitney U test on the per-unit yields and Cliff\textquotesingle s \ensuremath{\delta}, oriented so that a positive value means the benchmark is higher. DiffDock is the tool whose validity yield differs most between the sets, being cleaner on the experimental ligands with \ensuremath{\delta} = \ensuremath{-}0.49 at the frame-ligand unit and \ensuremath{-}0.68 once each ligand is collapsed to the median over its four frames, its experimental units sitting at a median of 100\% valid against a benchmark median of 80\%. That contrast does not survive multiplicity correction once the ligand rather than the frame-ligand pair is taken as the unit, at Holm p = 0.131 against the 0.009 obtained at the frame-ligand unit, so it is reported here as an effect size and a direction rather than as an established difference. AutoDock shows the smallest movement between the sets, \ensuremath{\delta} = \ensuremath{-}0.16 at the frame-ligand unit and \ensuremath{-}0.10 at the ligand unit with Holm p = 0.195 and 0.566, which is what its stability across the two sets would predict, with eleven fully clean units of twelve on the experimental side. EquiBind trends the other way, towards the benchmark, at \ensuremath{\delta} = +0.30 and +0.36 with Holm p = 0.135 and 0.556. The ligand-unit values are taken from the both-unit table of the ligand-level sensitivity analysis, which re-tests every Orai cross-panel contrast at both units.

The comparison is limited by the size and the chemistry of the experimental set, namely three ligands against 308, so the tests have little power and the non-significant results mean that no difference was detected rather than that none exists. Both EquiBind columns now use the same gnina refiner, so its cross-set contrast is no longer confounded by refiner choice. One earlier stage does still differ, because the experimental panel applied the in-protein UFF relaxation recorded in Table~\ref{tab:appendix-orai-protocols} and the control panel did not, and that relaxation deforms ligand geometry while relieving clash. The EquiBind cross-set contrast alone therefore carries a preparation difference as well as a ligand-set difference and is read here as descriptive only.

In summary, the ordering AutoDock Vina, DiffDock and EquiBind is preserved for physical validity in both Orai ligand panels. This is consistent with, but does not prove, transfer from the calibration benchmark because the experimental panel contains only three ligands. AutoDock output needs little geometric repair, DiffDock is mostly valid after smina refinement, and EquiBind requires strict validity screening even after gnina optimisation. On this metric no between-panel difference is established at the available power. The apparent DiffDock effect is the largest of the three and is reported descriptively, and with three experimental ligands none of the three contrasts survives multiplicity correction at the ligand unit.

\hypertarget{operational-placement-and-usable-yield}{%
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Operational Placement and Usable Yield }{Operational Placement and Usable Yield }}\label{operational-placement-and-usable-yield}}

The Orai1 target has no ligand-bound reference, so RMSD to a native pose cannot be computed. Accuracy is therefore replaced by an operational placement rule. Throughout this Orai1 section the AutoDock arm is the gnina-rescored variant, that is the Vina search followed by gnina minimisation and CNN rescoring, which is the strongest AutoDock variant on the calibration benchmark. It is written simply as AutoDock Vina for readability. This now holds for the experimental-versus-benchmark comparison figures as well, both panels being pinned to the same gnina-rescored variant, so no comparison in this chapter is made across different engines. A pose is counted as usable only when it is PoseBusters-valid, remains associated with the receptor surface and lies outside the R91-E106 transmembrane exclusion slab. This rule focuses the analysis on an outer-pore hypothesis. It is not a near-native analogue or experimental ground truth.

