\caption{Usable pose yield per frame-ligand unit. Each point is one Orai frame paired with one ligand}\label{fig:results-r13}
\end{figure}

Each tool is capped at its top ten ranked poses per unit and each unit contributes the yield of the poses it actually produced, so for any one tool the two ligand sets sit on one comparable axis. That denominator is not identical across tools, because DiffDock fell short of ten poses on 178 control units while the other two arms produced ten everywhere, which is why its benchmark usable median of 71.4\% falls on a seven-pose unit rather than on a multiple of ten. AutoDock Vina gives the highest usable yield on the benchmark ligands but not on the experimental ones, a benchmark median of 90\% of produced poses against an experimental median of 60\%, with interquartile ranges of 70 to 100\% and 40 to 72.5\% and means of 79.2\% and 55.0\%. DiffDock sits below it on the benchmark at 71.4\% and above it on the experimental ligands at 65.0\%, with spreads of 60 to 90\% and 60 to 70\% and means of 70.9\% and 68.3\%. EquiBind is degenerate in both sets, its median and both quartiles pinned at zero and its mean only 2.9\% on the benchmark and 0.8\% on the experimental ligands.

Tested at the frame-ligand unit, which removes the nesting of poses inside a unit although the units of one ligand remain correlated with each other, with an unpaired two-sided Mann-Whitney U and Cliff\textquotesingle s \ensuremath{\delta}, one tool separates the two ligand sets. AutoDock returns less on the experimental ligands than on the benchmark (U = 10803.0, \ensuremath{\delta} = +0.50) and survives Holm correction across the three tools (p = 0.005), and it is the one contrast in this chapter that also survives the collapse to the ligand unit, at \ensuremath{\delta} = +0.90 with a Holm-corrected p of 0.016 on ligand-collapsed medians of 60.0\% against 95.0\%, whereas DiffDock trends the same way without reaching significance (U = 8462.0, \ensuremath{\delta} = +0.16, p = 0.667) and EquiBind shows nothing (\ensuremath{\delta} = +0.02, p = 0.832). The pooled pose-level counterpart agrees. Its gaps are differences between pooled rates rather than between the per-unit means quoted above, which for DiffDock are not the same quantity because its per-unit pose counts vary. AutoDock\textquotesingle s gap of 24.2 percentage points, from 79.17\% on the benchmark ligands to 55.00\% on the experimental ones, gives a Fisher p below 1e-4 that survives correction, with DiffDock at 2.7 points (71.07\% against 68.33\%) and EquiBind at 2.0 points (2.86\% against 0.83\%) clearly non-significant. On this metric, therefore, AutoDock Vina is the only tool whose usable yield differs between the experimental ligands and the benchmark library, and the shortfall falls on the experimental set. With twelve correlated experimental units the tests have little power, so the two null results are an absence of detected difference rather than evidence that those sets behave equivalently.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.78\textheight,keepaspectratio]{media/media/image15.png}
\caption{Transmembrane loss of PoseBusters-valid poses, benchmark against experimental ligands. Panel (A) loss rate per tool as pooled pose-level proportions with Wilson 95\% intervals. Panel (B) the difference per tool as benchmark minus experimental, in percentage points}\label{fig:results-r14}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:results-r14} describes the share of already PoseBusters-valid poses that lies inside the transmembrane slab. All rates in this paragraph are taken on the same top-ten capped basis as Table~\ref{tab:results-orai-yield}, with the denominator the tool\textquotesingle s own PoseBusters-valid poses rather than a fixed pose count, so the differing control-panel totals of 12,010, 11,673 and 12,150 produced poses are already absorbed into it. AutoDock Vina loses 13.6\% of valid control-panel poses (1,499 of 11,007) and 44.5\% of experimental poses (53 of 119). DiffDock loses 9.6\% (881 of 9,177) and 26.8\% (30 of 112), respectively, while EquiBind loses 93.3\% (4,808 of 5,156) and 96.6\% (28 of 29 experimental valid poses). Because poses are clustered within frame-ligand units, these pose-level contrasts are descriptive and their nominal tests should not be treated as independent biological replication.

Figures~\ref{fig:results-r13} and \ref{fig:results-r14} separate validity from conditional placement. DiffDock loses a smaller share of its already valid experimental poses to the transmembrane slab than AutoDock Vina (26.8\% versus 44.5\%), and the same ordering holds on the control panel (9.6\% versus 13.6\%). On the experimental panel that conditional advantage is large enough to give DiffDock the higher net usable yield as well, even though AutoDock Vina generates more valid poses. EquiBind fails the placement requirement outside the transmembrane exclusion slab almost completely. Of its 29 valid experimental poses, 28 lie in the slab and only one is usable. The combined placement screen, rather than physical validity alone, is therefore decisive for this Orai1 protocol.

Two limits bound these comparisons. The transmembrane-loss rates are pooled pose-level tests that treat correlated poses within a unit as independent, so they overstate significance and are descriptive only. Their per-unit counterpart, the usable-yield test above, is significant for AutoDock Vina alone, so AutoDock\textquotesingle s 30.9-point benchmark-to-experimental loss gap is corroborated at the frame-ligand unit and again at the ligand unit, while the DiffDock and EquiBind gaps remain pooled descriptions. The cross-set differences are also reported as absolute differences rather than ratios, because the experimental denominator rests on three ligands and at most twelve units, which makes a ratio such as the apparent 0.31-fold reduction in loss unstable. Since the four frames are snapshots of one receptor, even the per-unit test is anti-conservative for the question of generalisation to new ligands.

\hypertarget{cross-tool-clustering-1}{%
\subsection{Cross-Tool Clustering}\label{cross-tool-clustering-1}}

The clustering approach is applied per MD frame and ligand pair, one pose cloud per frame, ligand and tool, to ask whether the three toolchains point to the same region of Orai and how well formed each tool\textquotesingle s own cloud is. Since frames of the same ligand are snapshots of one receptor-ligand system rather than independent observations, the pooled medians below are mildly pseudoreplicated. A frame-robust summary that collapses each ligand to the median over its frames first gives 30.0 \angstrom{} against the pooled 29.5 \angstrom{} on the benchmark, so that figure is stable under the aggregation, whereas on the experimental ligands it rises to 42.2 \angstrom{} against a pooled 30.1 \angstrom{}. The experimental median is therefore not stable under frame-robust aggregation, which is what three ligands and eleven usable pairs would predict, so only the benchmark value should be read as a settled central estimate.

