\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:results-r16} breaks the same agreement down by tool pair, giving the consensus-site distance in panel (A) and the percentage of pairs within the 5\angstrom{} threshold in panel (B). One pairing separates the ligand sets more than the others. Effect sizes on this distance endpoint are oriented so that a positive Cliff\textquotesingle s \ensuremath{\delta} means the experimental ligands sit further apart, which inverts the benchmark-higher orientation used for the yield and cluster-quality endpoints elsewhere in this chapter. DiffDock and EquiBind show the largest gap, 47.6\angstrom{} on the experimental ligands against 30.7\angstrom{} on the benchmark with \ensuremath{\delta} = +0.91. It is nominally significant at both units, at Holm p = 0.027 over three usable experimental pairs and 0.006 once each ligand is collapsed to the median over its frames, but the collapse leaves only two experimental ligands, since no Synta-66 EquiBind pose survived the transmembrane exclusion and the top-ten cap that feed the consensus clustering. Two ligands cannot support an inferential claim, so this is an exploratory effect estimated from very little data rather than a corrected finding. AutoDock against DiffDock (30.1\angstrom{} against 28.4\angstrom{}, \ensuremath{\delta} = +0.30, p = 0.275) and AutoDock against EquiBind (27.2\angstrom{} against 26.0\angstrom{}, \ensuremath{\delta} = +0.04, p = 0.900) remain flat. The agreement rates tell the more important story. AutoDock and DiffDock land within 5 \angstrom{} in 15\% of benchmark pairs, 184 of 1,189, and in none of the eleven usable experimental pairs, whereas every EquiBind pairing stays at or below 4\%, namely 2 of 468 against AutoDock and 19 of 481 against DiffDock, with none at all on the experimental ligands, and those rate differences give a Fisher p of 1.000. Pooled over all three pairings, agreement within 5 \angstrom{} is 10\% on the benchmark, 205 of 2,138, and none of the seventeen experimental pairs. Three-way agreement, in which all three pairwise distances of a unit fall within 5 \angstrom{} at once, is a far harder requirement than these per-pair rates, and it is reached in one of the 467 benchmark units that carry all three tools, 0.2\%, and in none of the three experimental ones. The only real agreement channel is therefore AutoDock against DiffDock, and any consensus site on Orai is in practice a question about those two tools alone.

