Figure~\ref{fig:results-r17} reports reference-free cluster quality per tool on Orai, the only form of cluster assessment available without a crystallographic site. AutoDock Vina forms the best-separated pose clouds in both ligand sets and the most compact ones on the benchmark, although on the experimental ligands DiffDock is the tighter of the two at a median within-cluster spread of 0.34 \angstrom{} against 0.62 \angstrom{}, lower being better. EquiBind forms the loosest clouds and often collapses into one diffuse mode. High bootstrap stability in such a degenerate case reflects reproducibility of the same broad cloud rather than accurate site resolution. The largest experimental-versus-control gap across the eighteen tool-by-metric cells is DiffDock\textquotesingle s silhouette, at a median of 0.17 on the experimental ligands against 0.42 on the benchmark, so that on the benchmark-higher orientation used for these quality metrics, Cliff\textquotesingle s \ensuremath{\delta} = +0.53 at the frame-ligand unit and +0.72 at the ligand unit. It reaches Holm p = 0.031 at the frame-ligand unit but falls to Holm p = 0.516 once each ligand is collapsed to the median over its four frames, so it is reported descriptively rather than as a corrected finding. The Calinski-Harabasz cell for the same tool moves the other way under that collapse, from Holm p = 0.074 to 0.031, which the ligand-level sensitivity analysis reported with these results flags as an artefact of removing the within-ligand spread rather than as new evidence, so it is not promoted here either. Everywhere else no set difference was detected at the available power, and the experimental panel contains only three ligands, so that absence does not establish equivalence or validate the control panel as a stand-in.

