\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:results-r18} pairs the raw pose cloud against the filtered cloud within each frame-ligand unit of the benchmark panel, across the three quality axes. The medians quoted here are those of the paired subset, that is the units that carry both clouds, which for AutoDock Vina and DiffDock is close to the full panel but for EquiBind is only 49 units on spread and 37 on stability out of 1,215, so the EquiBind values sit below the wider raw boxes drawn in the figure. Filtering to poses that are PoseBusters-valid and outside the transmembrane exclusion slab does not tighten the clouds. Compactness is essentially unmoved for every tool, AutoDock going from a median 0.55 to 0.53\angstrom{} at a rank-biserial of \ensuremath{-}0.09 (Holm-corrected p = 0.340), DiffDock from 0.57 to 0.57 (p = 1.000) and EquiBind from 1.30 to 1.20 (p = 1.000). What filtering does improve is reproducibility rather than tightness. Bootstrap stability rises significantly for all three, AutoDock from 0.77 to 0.78 at a rank-biserial of +0.25, DiffDock from 0.70 to 0.72 at +0.54 and EquiBind from 0.83 to 1.00 at +0.94, each with a corrected p below 1e-3, and DiffDock also gains on silhouette, from 0.43 to 0.47 at +0.30 and p below 1e-4. The EquiBind rise is degenerate rather than informative, since 27 of its 37 paired filtered clouds collapse to a single cluster, a case in which the bootstrap Jaccard stability is identically 1.00 by construction, and on the ten paired units that keep more than one cluster the medians move only from 0.64 to 0.71. Filtering therefore does not detectably tighten the wide scatter along the pore, so on this evidence it is not an artefact of invalid or mislocated poses that filtering could clean away. No equivalence test was run, so this bounds the effect of filtering rather than establishing that the scatter is intrinsic to how the tools sample, and the EquiBind contrast in particular rests on few surviving units. The surviving AutoDock and DiffDock poses do partition more reproducibly.

In summary, the tools do not provide a reliable consensus site on Orai1. Pairwise sites differ by a pooled median of roughly 30 \angstrom{} on both ligand sets, rising to 42 \angstrom{} on the experimental ligands once each ligand is collapsed to the median over its frames, and agreement within 5 \angstrom{} reaches at most 15\%. AutoDock Vina forms the best-separated pose clouds, and the tightest on the benchmark, but precision is not evidence of accuracy without a reference. The three largest experimental-versus-control gaps among these clustering and agreement proxies are DiffDock\textquotesingle s higher physical-validity yield, its larger consensus-site distance from EquiBind and its lower silhouette, and none of them is established on all three ligands once the ligand rather than the frame-ligand pair is taken as the unit, the validity and silhouette gaps falling well short of correction and the consensus-site gap surviving but resting on two experimental ligands. The one cross-panel contrast that survives that collapse at both units is AutoDock Vina\textquotesingle s lower usable yield, which is a placement rather than a clustering proxy and is reported with the placement results above. The largely preserved descriptive ordering supports cautious use of the control panel for these proxies, but it does not validate equivalence.

