\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.78\textheight,keepaspectratio]{media/media/image20.png}
\caption{Total protein-ligand contacts per pose on Orai}\label{fig:results-r19}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:results-r19} compares total contacts per pose between the two ligand sets. The pose-level median is printed on each box. AutoDock Vina trends toward denser contacts for the experimental ligands, its median rising from 26 for the benchmark set to 28 for the experimental set, and DiffDock moves the same way, from 15 to 18. Neither trend survives testing one level up on the per-complex mean, which removes the pose pseudoreplication. AutoDock gives Cliff\textquotesingle s \ensuremath{\delta} = \ensuremath{-}0.20 at a Holm-adjusted p = 0.254 at the frame-ligand unit (per-complex-mean medians 25.7 for the benchmark against 29.7 for the experimental set), and DiffDock \ensuremath{\delta} = \ensuremath{-}0.27 at a Holm-adjusted p = 0.205 at the same unit (15.7 against 18.2). Collapsing each ligand to the median over its four frames weakens both further, to \ensuremath{\delta} = \ensuremath{-}0.20 for AutoDock and \ensuremath{-}0.19 for DiffDock, each at a Holm-adjusted p = 1.000. EquiBind contributes only one usable experimental pose, which is too few to test, so it is left out of the correction family rather than counted as a hypothesis. No difference in total contact count is detected at the available power, which is not evidence that the panels have equivalent contact intensity.

