\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:results-r22} summarises how far the two libraries agree on the residues they engage. Overlap is graded by tool, the residue-level Jaccard being 0.56 for AutoDock and 0.31 for DiffDock, and for AutoDock it falls to 0.49 once the interaction type must match as well, since the two libraries hit the same residues without always forming the same kind of contact there, which is the fingerprint-level echo of the hydrogen-bond against hydrophobic shift above. DiffDock moves the same way but less far, from 0.31 to 0.27. Both Jaccards are taken on the same 10\% characteristic-contact threshold, so the residue-to-typed drop measures a change of contact type rather than a change of basis. An earlier reading, on which AutoDock fell to 0.28 and DiffDock rose to 0.35, compared a thresholded residue set against an unthresholded typed one, and most of the apparent AutoDock drop was the long tail of one-off typed contacts that a benchmark set of several thousand poses accumulates. AutoDock shares 33 characteristic residues between the libraries and DiffDock 13, whereas EquiBind shares none, its single experimental pose leaving a Jaccard of zero that carries no meaning. One reading trap deserves flagging. The 10\% threshold is an absolute count, roughly four poses for the experimental set against several hundred for the benchmark, and the diverse benchmark ligands spread their contacts across many residues while the three repeatedly docked modulators concentrate theirs, so the experimental-only count exceeding the benchmark-only count, 16 against 10 for AutoDock, is an artefact of set size and homogeneity rather than evidence of promiscuity. The shared set and the per-residue frequencies are the robust readings.

The surviving modulator poses show partial concordance with published pharmacology. Synta-66 has been mapped to the outer TM1/TM3 interface adjacent to the E106 selectivity filter \cite{ref070}, \cite{ref071}. AutoDock Vina contacts Ala177 in 10 of its 31 modulator poses against 25.6\% of its benchmark poses, an uncorrected p = 0.410 that is compatible with TM3 involvement without establishing it. DiffDock offers no support on this point, since its modulator poses contact Ala177 in a single pose of 36 against 16.2\% of its benchmark poses. Glu106 itself separates neither tool, being touched by one modulator pose under each, so the TM3 side of the filter-adjacent hypothesis rests on the AutoDock arm alone and the filter residue itself is supported by neither. These observations make the poses biologically interpretable, but contacts contributed by different tools cannot be combined to validate one binding mode. The remaining frequent residues define plausible hypotheses for mutagenesis or ligand-bound simulation.

The comparison is unpaired and confounded by chemistry, so the contact-type split characterises chemotypes rather than tool skill. Without a cognate structure, literature concordance remains partial and some retained poses are still anatomically implausible. Pose-level residue tests are pseudoreplicated, the experimental arm contains three ligands, and EquiBind contributes only one usable experimental pose. Effect sizes and qualitative agreement with pharmacology therefore carry more weight than nominal p-values.

Taken together, the Orai1 analysis inverts the ordering of its own control panel on net usable yield. AutoDock Vina leads that control panel, but on the experimental ligands DiffDock + smina is ahead, which is also the ordering calibration accuracy gives, where DiffDock + smina has the higher recovery point estimate. AutoDock Vina is physically clean yet retains only 50 to 60\% of poses per ligand under the operational placement rule, with a median usable yield of 60\% across the twelve frame--ligand units. DiffDock leads at a median frame--ligand-unit validity of 100\% and usable yield of 65\%. Conditional on already valid poses, it also has a lower transmembrane-loss rate than AutoDock Vina on both panels. EquiBind has a median experimental frame--ligand-unit validity of 5\%, and 28 of its 29 valid poses fail the transmembrane exclusion rule, leaving one usable pose out of 120.

Three methodological conclusions follow. First, chemical validity alone overstates usefulness on a channel target, since a tool can be geometrically flawless and still dock into the ion-conduction pathway, so usable yield rather than raw validity is the defensible way to summarise performance on this target and the placement filter is what exposes EquiBind. Second, cross-tool consensus is not available as a confidence signal here, because the three tools disagree about where these ligands bind by a pooled median of roughly 30 \angstrom{} in both ligand sets, agree within 5 \angstrom{} in at best 15\% of pairs, with all three agreeing at once in a single benchmark unit of 467 and in none of the experimental ones, and filtering to valid poses that satisfy the placement rule outside the transmembrane exclusion slab does not detectably tighten that scatter, so it is not obviously an artefact of invalid or mislocated poses, although no equivalence test was run, so that filtering result bounds the effect rather than establishing that the scatter is intrinsic to how the tools sample an elongated pore. Third, the surviving poses do carry chemical signal. Both libraries engage the same pocket residues, yet the real modulators are descriptively more hydrophobic and halogen-rich and less hydrogen-bonded than the benchmark ligands. Both tools place the modulators on the extracellular TM1 and loop residues His113, Tyr115 and Leu202, which both retain in their shared fingerprints. The TM3 sub-pocket lined by Arg170, Leu174 and Ala177 is an AutoDock observation alone, since none of those three residues survives into DiffDock\textquotesingle s much smaller shared set, and it is the AutoDock arm that places the modulators in the outer TM1 and TM3 region adjacent to the selectivity filter where the Synta-66 site has been mapped experimentally. Because contacts contributed by different tools cannot be combined to validate one binding mode, that concordance is a single-tool observation and not a two-tool consensus. AutoDock also puts a separate cluster of contacts on the cytosolic N-terminal segment below TM1, at Lys85 and its neighbours, which is the fourteenth of the fifteen residues drawn in Figure~\ref{fig:results-r21}. That cluster lies on the opposite face of the membrane from the extracellular site, so it supports no part of the Synta-66 concordance and is noted only for completeness.

The cross-panel comparison is reassuring only in a limited descriptive sense. Three experimental-versus-control contrasts survive correction once the ligand rather than the frame-ligand pair is taken as the unit, and exactly one of them also survives at the frame-ligand unit, namely AutoDock Vina\textquotesingle s lower usable yield on the experimental modulators, at ligand-collapsed medians of 60.0\% against 95.0\% with Cliff\textquotesingle s \ensuremath{\delta} of +0.90 and a Holm-corrected p of 0.016. Being significant at both units and resting on all three experimental ligands together makes it the most robust cross-panel observation in this chapter. The remaining large gaps do not survive. DiffDock\textquotesingle s higher physical-validity yield and its lower silhouette on the experimental ligands both fall well short of correction under the collapse, the DiffDock against EquiBind inter-tool distance survives but rests on two experimental ligands, and the interaction-type family behaves the same way, with none of its thirty tool-by-type contrasts surviving Benjamini-Hochberg correction at the ligand unit. Across all sixty contrasts three reach an adjusted p below 0.05 at the ligand unit, against nineteen at the frame-ligand unit, and the ligand-level sensitivity analysis flags one of those three, DiffDock\textquotesingle s Calinski-Harabasz index, as an artefact of the collapse, since it does not reach correction at the frame-ligand unit at all. Every comparison in this section is nonetheless reported descriptively, as medians, per-ligand values and Cliff\textquotesingle s \ensuremath{\delta}, because three ligands and four snapshots of one receptor cannot support corrected inference in either direction, and that caution applies to the surviving usable-yield contrast as much as to the null ones. Interaction-type differences are consistent with the more hydrophobic, halogen-rich experimental chemotypes, and residue-level fingerprint overlap is moderate for AutoDock Vina at a Jaccard of 0.56 and weaker for DiffDock at 0.31. For Orai1, the practical recommendation is to retain AutoDock Vina and refined DiffDock as separate hypothesis generators, prioritise poses that satisfy the validity and placement rules and agree with independent pharmacology, and omit EquiBind in its present configuration. Sparse overlap between the two viable tools should not itself be treated as validation.

\protect\hypertarget{_Toc235735941}{}{}

\hypertarget{dataset-summary}{%
\section{Dataset Summary}\label{dataset-summary}}

Across the 303 common calibration complexes retained from the 308-complex source benchmark, all three pipelines searched the whole receptor without any crystal-derived binding-site information, so the comparison is matched on search scope. Under those conditions the endpoints separate rather than agree. AutoDock Vina + gnina is the strongest pipeline on physical validity, at a pooled 99.5\% against 84.5\% for DiffDock + smina and 51.8\% for unguided EquiBind + gnina. DiffDock + smina has the highest point estimate on the headline metric, recovering a PoseBusters-valid pose within 2\angstrom{} for 34.0\% of complexes at rank-1 against 29.4\% for AutoDock Vina + gnina, and retains the higher point estimate at every ranking depth and on conformational form as well. Its rank-1 near-nativeness advantage of +4.6 percentage points carries a 95\% interval of \ensuremath{-}2.2 to +11.4 points, so that contrast is inconclusive rather than settled in either direction, as set out in the Results (Near-Nativeness and Form). DiffDock still carries substantial selection headroom, because acceptable poses often occur within a shallow top-15 pool but are not ranked first, although most of its rank-1 failures remain unrecovered even at that depth. EquiBind remains weakest and is limited chiefly by generation, localisation and raw geometry. On Orai1, DiffDock + smina has the highest net usable yield on the experimental ligands and also loses the smaller conditional share of already valid poses to the transmembrane slab, while AutoDock Vina leads the control panel.

The ordering therefore depends on which property is being asked about, and no single tool is best on all of them. That is a different conclusion from the one a crystal-boxed comparison would support, and it is discussed further below.

Two conclusions cut across the datasets. First, post-hoc minimisation makes many learned poses physically admissible but does little to correct gross placement. Second, the residual bottleneck differs by tool. DiffDock leaves substantial ranking headroom, while EquiBind often fails to sample a usable placement at all. It is therefore not accurate to describe all remaining loss as selection rather than search.

The calibration analysis carries the greater inferential weight because it contains 303 crystal-referenced complexes. The Orai1 arm contains three ligands and twelve correlated frame-ligand units without a cognate pose. Its value lies in reference-free workflow diagnosis and partial concordance with filter-adjacent pharmacology, not in a statistically powered estimate of prospective accuracy.

\hypertarget{pose-production-costs}{%
\section{Pose Production Costs}\label{pose-production-costs}}

Accuracy and validity say nothing about what a result costs. The relevant efficiency measure is not throughput but the compute spent per qualifying result, so cost is expressed per pose that clears the deployment bar, namely PoseBusters-valid and within 2 \angstrom{} of the crystal ligand. Two views follow, the wall-clock a user waits for and the hardware resource a cluster bills. All docking ran on a single workstation. That machine used an Intel Core i9-14900HX processor with 32 hardware threads, an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4070 Laptop GPU with 8 GB of video memory, and 32 GB of system memory. The AutoDock Vina search ran multi-threaded on the processor at exhaustiveness 32, while the DiffDock and EquiBind inference passes ran on the GPU. The refinement stages split the other way. The gnina rescoring that completes the AutoDock arm ran on the GPU, whereas the refinement that completes the EquiBind arm is accounted as processor work and dominates that arm\textquotesingle s bill, so neither of those two pipelines sits on a single device.

Every figure in this section is measured on the whole-protein run, over the 302 timing-complete complexes, which are all but one of the 303 complexes carried through the accuracy analysis, the exception being 7WCF\_ACP, and the AutoDock arm is AutoDock Vina + gnina with both stages timed. The two stages are worth separating, because they are not comparable in size. The blind Vina search itself took 2.21 wall-hours across the 302 complexes, against 1.16 hours for the crystal-ligand-boxed search over the 293 complexes the two runs share. Those two totals are not a like-for-like ratio, because they cover different complex sets, and restricted to the 293 shared complexes they stand at 2.15 against 1.16 hours, a ratio of totals of 1.9. The quoted cost is instead the paired per-complex statistic, whose median is 2.3 with an interquartile range of 1.8 to 3.1. It exceeds the ratio of totals because a total weights every complex by its own cost and the most expensive boxed complexes are the ones on which the two runs differ least, at Spearman\textquotesingle s \ensuremath{\rho} = \ensuremath{-}0.72 between a complex\textquotesingle s boxed cost and its blind-to-boxed ratio. Withdrawing the search box therefore costs a median factor of 2.3 per complex, less than the enlargement of the search volume would suggest because exhaustiveness rather than volume sets the sampling budget. The gnina rescoring pass then added a further 12.13 hours, so the learned rescorer is roughly five and a half times more expensive than the docking it re-ranks and dominates the AutoDock cost line entirely.\footnote{Rescoring runs one pose at a time on the GPU, whereas Vina spreads each dock over 32 CPU threads, so the two stages are billed in different currencies, the search costing 70.61 CPU-core-hours and the rescoring 12.13 GPU-hours. Optimiser time is recovered from the per-complex optimiser logs, where cached entries carry the originally measured elapsed time in their provenance sidecar rather than the cost of the cache lookup.}

Figure~\ref{fig:results-r23} gives the wall-clock cost per qualifying pose, one value per complex, over the complexes for which a tool produced at least one such pose.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=0.78\textheight,keepaspectratio]{media/media/image24.png}
\caption{Wall-clock seconds per qualifying pose, where a qualifying pose is PoseBusters-valid and within 2 \angstrom{} of the crystal ligand. Each box summarises only the complexes for which that tool produced a qualifying pose, namely 151, 168 and 62, whereas the bracketed paired tests use the 32 complexes on which all three succeed}\label{fig:results-r23}
\end{figure}

The cost ordering under a blind search is the reverse of the boxed run. Medians are 119.7 s per qualifying pose for AutoDock Vina + gnina (interquartile range 91.2 to 138.8 s, 151 complexes), 50.9 s for DiffDock (15.8 to 133.8 s, 168 complexes) and 8.5 s for EquiBind (3.2 to 22.1 s, 62 complexes). On the 32 complexes where all three succeed the paired tests confirm that ordering (Friedman \ensuremath{\chi}\textsuperscript{2} = 39.8, p = 2.3e-09, Kendall W = 0.62, every pair surviving Holm correction), with DiffDock costing 0.12 times as much per usable pose as AutoDock and EquiBind 0.09 times. On the boxed run the same endpoint, that is a PoseBusters-valid pose within 2 \angstrom{} of the crystal ligand, put AutoDock cheapest at 3.5 s, although the boxed AutoDock arm is the raw Vina search with no gnina pass rather than the Vina + gnina pipeline measured here. Between the two runs the classical engine moves from the cheapest pipeline to the most expensive, by a factor of 34, but the box alone does not produce that move. Three effects compound to produce it, and the largest of them is not the box. The blind search costs 2.3 times more per complex, its gnina pass adds a further five and a half times that again, and the resulting cost is then amortised over fewer qualifying poses, because the median number of near-native valid poses per qualifying complex falls from two in the boxed run to one in the blind run while the number of complexes yielding any such pose falls from 259 to 151. Each factor is the median of its own distribution, so together they indicate the size and direction of the three contributions rather than an exact accounting that multiplies out to 34.

Cost climbs as the quality bar tightens, most steeply for AutoDock. From any generated pose through valid to valid and near-native the median runs 4.3, 4.4 and 119.7 s for AutoDock Vina + gnina, 8.1, 9.8 and 50.9 s for DiffDock and 0.9, 1.2 and 8.5 s for EquiBind, while the contributing complexes fall from 302 to 302 and 151 for AutoDock, from 302 to 299 and 168 for DiffDock, and from 302 to 247 and 62 for EquiBind. Fewer qualifying poses spread the same fixed docking wall further, and many complexes yield none at all. This is where AutoDock\textquotesingle s near-perfect validity stops helping, since almost every pose it produces is admissible but only a minority are near-native, so the tightening from valid to valid-and-near-native multiplies its unit cost by a factor of about 27.5 against about 5.2 for DiffDock, whose median rises from 9.8 to 50.9 s. EquiBind\textquotesingle s competitive unit cost remains an artefact of its failures, since its 8.5 s is a median over the 62 complexes in which it succeeds and it fails in the remaining 240.

Figure~\ref{fig:results-r24} removes parallelism from the comparison, reporting the hardware resource consumed per qualifying pose separately for CPU and GPU, on the same near-native and valid criterion as Figure~\ref{fig:results-r23}.

