\caption{Hardware resource per near-native valid pose, that is per pose that is PoseBusters-valid and within 2 \angstrom{} of the crystal ligand}\label{fig:results-r24}
\end{figure}

On this axis the blind search separates the tools even more sharply. AutoDock Vina + gnina consumes 1,171.4 CPU-core-seconds and 201.3 GPU-seconds per qualifying pose, DiffDock 50.4 GPU-seconds and 2.97 CPU-core-seconds, and EquiBind 846.5 CPU-core-seconds with 2.1 GPU-seconds. AutoDock is now the most expensive pipeline on every axis at once, having lost the wall-clock advantage that its 32-thread parallelism used to buy it while retaining the CPU-core-second bill that parallelism always implied. The denominators explain much of the gap, since the three tools convert their compute into 217 (AutoDock Vina + gnina), 1,602 (DiffDock) and 305 (EquiBind) qualifying poses. For both DiffDock and EquiBind the GPU time is the docking itself, whereas AutoDock\textquotesingle s GPU time is entirely its gnina rescoring pass, which nonetheless carries the largest per-qualifying-pose GPU bill of the three because its yield of qualifying poses is so small.

Three caveats bound these figures. The wall-clock comparison is cross-hardware, AutoDock running multi-threaded on CPU against a single GPU for the learned tools, so it measures elapsed time rather than identical work. The resource values are order-of-magnitude costs, since AutoDock\textquotesingle s CPU-core-seconds cover only the Vina search and assume the full 32 threads are occupied, while its gnina pass is accounted separately as GPU occupancy, and EquiBind\textquotesingle s wall time is a best-configuration estimate. The resource panel carries no inferential test by design, being a pooled aggregate rather than a per-complex distribution. The tools nevertheless differ sharply in whether a timing-complete complex yields any qualifying pose, namely 151 of 302 for AutoDock Vina + gnina, 168 of 302 for DiffDock + smina and 62 of 302 for EquiBind + gnina (Cochran\textquotesingle s Q = 99.2, p = 2.9\ensuremath{\times}10\textsuperscript{\ensuremath{-}22}).

In summary, under the blind whole-protein configuration that supplies every other result in this chapter, AutoDock Vina + gnina is the most expensive pipeline per usable pose on the wall-clock axis and on both hardware currencies, and it is not the cheapest on any currency per pose generated either. Per generated pose EquiBind is the cheapest on wall-clock time at 0.89 seconds against 5.73 for AutoDock and 9.53 for DiffDock, and on GPU time at 0.07 seconds against 4.85 and 9.00, while DiffDock is the cheapest on CPU-core time at 0.53 seconds against 28.20 for AutoDock and 28.50 for EquiBind, so AutoDock sits mid-field on wall-clock and GPU time and is effectively tied with EquiBind at the expensive end of the CPU-core axis. Its advantage is coverage and admissibility rather than economy, since it returned at least one PoseBusters-valid pose for every one of the 302 complexes and qualifying poses for 151 of them, against 168 for DiffDock and 62 for EquiBind. The ordering also depends on whether poses or docking jobs carry the cost. Per attempted docking the median wall-clock is 129.5 s for AutoDock Vina + gnina against 241.7 s for DiffDock and 25.6 s for EquiBind, and charging the whole campaign to the complexes that yielded a qualifying pose gives 342, 509 and 130 s, so on that denominator AutoDock Vina + gnina is the cheaper of the two leading pipelines on wall-clock and on GPU-seconds, at 289 s per success against 480, while remaining far more expensive on CPU-core-seconds. Because all three tools produce about thirty poses per complex, this is the per-generated-pose view above rescaled rather than an independent measurement, and it does not alter the per-qualifying-pose result. DiffDock is the most cost-effective route to a near-native valid pose in this configuration, and EquiBind\textquotesingle s low median elapsed cost remains conditional on the 62 complexes in which it succeeds at all. Hardware-resource conclusions remain currency-specific. GPU time dominates the cost for DiffDock, which nonetheless also spends 2.97 CPU-core-seconds per qualifying pose, whereas CPU time dominates AutoDock Vina and EquiBind, with the qualification that AutoDock also carries a substantial GPU bill in its gnina rescoring pass. The efficiency ordering reported here therefore reverses the one obtained under a crystal-derived search box, which is itself the practical lesson, since a site-restricted benchmark flatters the classical engine on cost as well as on accuracy.

\hypertarget{discussion}{%
\chapter{\texorpdfstring{Discussion }{Discussion }}\label{discussion}}

\hypertarget{principal-findings-and-scope-of-inference}{%
\section{Principal Findings and Scope of Inference}\label{principal-findings-and-scope-of-inference}}

This study compared AutoDock Vina, DiffDock and EquiBind in two deliberately different settings, namely crystallographic re-docking on 303 calibration complexes retained from the 308-complex PoseBusters benchmark, and reference-free docking of three Orai1 modulators into four molecular-dynamics snapshots. The calibration benchmark provides the stronger evidence because every prediction can be tested against an experimental pose. In that setting the endpoints separate rather than electing a single winner. AutoDock Vina was the most reliable configured pipeline on physical validity, whereas refined DiffDock had the highest point estimate on the headline metric and matched or exceeded AutoDock on native-pocket recovery at every ranking depth once the search box was removed. Its near-nativeness advantage over AutoDock was inconclusive at the tested depths, because the paired intervals reported in the Results (Near-Nativeness and Form) exclude neither a null difference nor a lead of up to roughly thirteen percentage points, the widest upper bound among the four depths. Refined EquiBind was limited by low raw physical validity and weak in-place accuracy and pocket localisation. The Orai1 case preserved that split on physical validity but reversed it on usable yield, where refined DiffDock retained more poses than AutoDock Vina under the study\textquotesingle s operational validity and placement rules, and the absence of a ligand-bound Orai1 structure means that it cannot establish which predicted binding mode is correct.

The appropriate interpretation is therefore narrower than a general contest between artificial intelligence and physics. The results show that under this study\textquotesingle s preparation, search, ranking, refinement and evaluation settings the endpoints split between the two leading pipelines, with AutoDock Vina ahead on physical validity and refined DiffDock-L, the larger retrained DiffDock release used throughout this work \cite{ref091}, having higher point estimates for in-place accuracy, recovery and pocket localisation, although that in-place advantage over AutoDock is inconclusive at the tested depths on the interval evidence given in the Results (Near-Nativeness and Form). Both remain ahead of the tested EquiBind pipeline throughout. They do not show that classical docking universally outperforms current AI docking. EquiBind and the evaluated DiffDock version represent earlier learned architectures, while more recent diffusion, co-folding and hybrid systems report better performance when pockets are specified, receptor flexibility is represented and structural relaxation is included \cite{ref012}, \cite{ref013}, \cite{ref031}, \cite{ref064}, \cite{ref069}. Conversely, current similarity-controlled analyses continue to show that aggregate accuracy can conceal reliance on training-set analogues and weak extrapolation to new pockets \cite{ref035}, \cite{ref063}, \cite{ref065}, \cite{ref069}. The principal contribution of this thesis is thus its separation of failure modes, namely sampling, ranking, physical validity and localisation, rather than a permanent ranking of method classes.

The term physics-based also requires care. AutoDock Vina uses an empirical scoring function and an explicit search-and-optimisation procedure. It is not a full molecular-mechanics or free-energy simulation. In this thesis, physics-based is therefore an operational contrast with end-to-end learned pose generation. That distinction matters when interpreting the strong Vina result. It supports this classical search-and-score workflow, not a claim that a complete physical model of binding was evaluated.

\hypertarget{physical-validity-and-post-docking-optimisation}{%
\section{Physical Validity and Post-Docking Optimisation}\label{physical-validity-and-post-docking-optimisation}}

The largest initial separation between the tools occurred before RMSD was considered. AutoDock Vina + gnina produced 99.5\% PoseBusters-valid poses, against 98.2\% for the unrescored search, compared with 24.4\% for raw DiffDock and 2.9\% for raw unguided EquiBind. Refinement substantially changed the usability of the learned outputs, because smina raised DiffDock validity to 84.5\%, while gnina raised unguided EquiBind validity to 51.8\%. These gains support the central PoseBusters argument that RMSD alone is insufficient, because a geometrically close ligand can still contain unrealistic bond geometry, steric overlap or intermolecular contacts \cite{ref035}. They are also consistent with broader benchmarking in which learned methods can lead on pose accuracy while classical methods retain an advantage in structural rationality, making combined accuracy-and-validity criteria more discriminating than RMSD alone \cite{ref013}, \cite{ref032}.

Refinement did not have the same practical effect for the two learned methods. For DiffDock, the principal gain was conversion of already sampled hypotheses into physically admissible structures. Holding each pose at its ranked position, optimisation changed the per-rank near-native rate by only about 0.1 to 0.6 percentage points with smina and 0.1 to 0.7 with gnina, while substantially increasing PoseBusters validity. For EquiBind, the same rate rose more, though still modestly, by approximately 2.5 to 2.6 percentage points with smina and 2.8 to 3.1 with gnina, alongside a much larger validity gain. Table~\ref{tab:appendix-refinement-bands} lists these fixed-rank changes by pose-rank band. Local minimisation therefore repaired geometry far more effectively than it corrected blind placement. It could not recover a binding mode that had not been sampled or move a grossly mislocated ligand into the correct pocket.

The selected refiner should be treated as an empirical pipeline choice rather than a demonstrated mechanism. On the variant-selection endpoint the two DiffDock refiners are separated by at most two complexes, at 76 against 74 at rank-1 and 133 against 132 at the top-15 depth, and are effectively tied, whereas EquiBind + gnina clearly outperformed EquiBind + smina. Descriptions such as gentle preservation or aggressive repair are plausible interpretations of these observations, but the study did not directly measure optimisation trajectories or validate refiner selection on an independent dataset. Selecting and evaluating a variant on the same benchmark can overstate the portability of that choice.

Physical validity is also not equivalent to binding stability. Passing all PoseBusters checks (Table~\ref{tab:appendix-posebusters-checks}) removes important chemical, conformational and steric failures, but it does not establish favourable binding free energy, correct protonation and tautomer states, appropriate water or ion mediation, or persistence in a membrane environment. The thesis therefore supports a two-stage interpretation. Physical validity is a necessary admissibility filter, while native-like placement, interaction recovery and prospective experimental evidence provide separate tests of accuracy.

\hypertarget{sampling-ranking-and-interaction-recovery}{%
\section{Sampling, Ranking and Interaction Recovery}\label{sampling-ranking-and-interaction-recovery}}

The ranking-depth analysis separates pose generation from pose selection. Under the headline metric, a PoseBusters-valid pose within 2 \angstrom{} of the crystal ligand, rank-1 recovery was approximately 34.0\% for DiffDock + smina, 29.4\% for AutoDock Vina + gnina and 15.2\% for EquiBind + gnina. Retrospective best-of-top-15 recovery rose to approximately 55.1\%, 49.8\% and 20.1\%, respectively. The large DiffDock gain shows that useful poses were often generated but not assigned the highest confidence. AutoDock Vina gains almost as much in absolute terms, at 62 complexes against 64, so under blind conditions the classical engine is selection-limited to a comparable degree rather than being a reliable self-ranker. EquiBind gained only about five percentage points, indicating a predominantly sampling or placement limitation. No re-ranker can recover a binding mode that is absent from the pool.

The form-versus-in-place decomposition supports this diagnosis. At the prespecified 1 \angstrom{} Kabsch threshold, rank-1 form recovery was 52.1\% for DiffDock + smina, 46.9\% for AutoDock Vina + gnina and 30.7\% for EquiBind + gnina. At the best-of-top-30 depth it reached 74.6\%, 71.0\% and 49.8\%, respectively. DiffDock therefore has the highest point estimate for internal conformational credibility as well as for near-nativeness, although the latter advantage over AutoDock remains inconclusive at the tested depths on the interval evidence given in the Results (Near-Nativeness and Form). What it fails to do is rank the credible pose first rather than generate it. EquiBind was weaker on both axes. The distinction is chemically important because a pose can preserve ligand shape yet present its pharmacophore to the wrong residues.

Pocket recovery was easier than exact pose recovery, but under the whole-protein search it is no longer a settled contest. At rank-1, DiffDock reached the crystallographic pocket cluster for 55\% of complexes, AutoDock Vina for 53\% and EquiBind for 41\%, a spread narrow enough that the AutoDock and DiffDock confidence intervals overlap. By top-15 the corresponding rates were 82\%, 76\% and 49\%, so DiffDock has the higher point estimate wherever ranking depth is allowed to grow, and at that depth the reach advantage over AutoDock is separable on the paired test given in the Results (Cross-Tool Clustering). Pooling the three tools placed the correct pocket somewhere in the combined pose cloud for 93\% of complexes, yet the best inexpensive cluster-ranking rule selected it first in only 55\%. That shortfall is far larger than the equivalent gap under the superseded crystal-boxed search, where an already localised AutoDock made the pooled site obvious. It is evidence of a site-selection gap in the pooled ensemble, not evidence that exact pose prediction is 93\% solved. Cluster membership is a coarser endpoint than a physically valid pose within 2 \angstrom{}.

The interaction analysis provides an orthogonal check, and it now rests on the same whole-protein search as the rest of the chapter. On the top-5 union of typed contacts DiffDock + smina has the highest mean fingerprint overlap with the crystal ligand (0.45, compared with 0.37 for EquiBind + gnina and 0.36 for AutoDock Vina + gnina), reversing the boxed run on which the site-restricted AutoDock search reached 0.67. That reversal is a property of the AutoDock protocol rather than of the contact chemistry, since the two pipelines that docked blindly in both runs barely move between them, EquiBind + gnina returning 0.37 in each and DiffDock + smina shifting only from 0.44 to 0.45, against the halving of the AutoDock value. The search box is not isolated within that protocol, because the boxed AutoDock arm was raw AutoDock Vina while the blind one is AutoDock Vina + gnina. On the pose a user would actually take the tools are not separable at all, with a rank-1 F1 of 0.53 for both DiffDock and AutoDock Vina against 0.46 for EquiBind, which matches the placement result, where the two pipelines could likewise not be ordered, although only that placement contrast was tested against an equivalence margin and neither the interaction nor the pocket-cluster endpoint supports a claim that the two perform alike. Neither endpoint shows that they perform alike, because in both cases the data are inconclusive rather than positively equivalent. The separation appears with depth instead, where AutoDock decays to 0.36 by rank 5 while DiffDock holds near 0.51. DiffDock\textquotesingle s confidence ranking shows no detected association with interaction recovery, so that flat and comparatively good pose pool is left unexploited, whereas AutoDock\textquotesingle s gnina ranking orders its poses well but has genuinely worse ones to order. EquiBind missed more native contacts than it recovered at every rank. Ranking quality is therefore endpoint- and distribution-dependent. The improved cross-docking ranker in gnina 1.3, together with its less uniform redocking behaviour, makes the same point. An external score should be validated for the intended task rather than assumed to improve every setting \cite{ref067}.

\hypertarget{generalisation-in-the-context-of-current-literature}{%
\section{Generalisation in the Context of Current Literature}\label{generalisation-in-the-context-of-current-literature}}

The post-2021 construction of the PoseBusters benchmark reduces direct temporal overlap with the training data of early learned models, but it does not eliminate similarity leakage. A later crystal structure may contain a homologous receptor, a familiar pocket geometry or a close ligand analogue. PoseBusters reported a marked decline for receptors with low sequence identity to training proteins \cite{ref035}, and the later multidimensional analysis by Li et al. identified binding-pocket novelty as a central generalisation bottleneck \cite{ref013}. TEMPL further illustrates why a time split alone is not decisive. A simple template-transfer baseline achieved meaningful performance on a conventional PDBbind split, with substantially better results when related protein-ligand templates were available \cite{ref065}.

The 2026 PoseBench study sharpens this qualification. Modern co-folding systems generally outperformed comparable conventional and learned docking baselines overall, but they remained challenged by new protein-ligand binding poses and by the simultaneous demands of structural accuracy and chemical specificity \cite{ref069}. PoseX similarly reported that recent AI and co-folding systems can outperform physics-based baselines on self- and cross-docking after relaxation, and that pocket information and physics-inspired post-processing materially improve their outputs \cite{ref012}. These results do not contradict the present thesis. They show that conclusions depend on model generation, benchmark novelty, receptor information, flexibility and relaxation. The poor EquiBind result is consistent with current benchmarks that place early one-shot models below newer diffusion and co-folding systems. The DiffDock result identifies limitations of this configured version\textquotesingle s blind placement, chemical validity and confidence ranking rather than an intrinsic ceiling on generative docking.

The thesis therefore provides a useful bridge between two apparently opposing strands of literature. Studies such as PoseBusters, TEMPL and PoseBench warn that physical plausibility and generalisation can lag behind headline RMSD \cite{ref035}, \cite{ref065}, \cite{ref069}. PoseX and newer hybrid systems show that these deficiencies are not immutable when models receive pocket information and physics-informed relaxation \cite{ref012}, \cite{ref064}. The present results support both propositions. Raw learned output was often unusable, while post-processing rescued many structures but did not remove sampling and ranking errors.

\hypertarget{interpretation-of-the-reference-free-orai1-study}{%
\section{Interpretation of the Reference-Free Orai1 Study}\label{interpretation-of-the-reference-free-orai1-study}}

The Orai1 analysis addresses a more realistic but less identifiable problem. AutoDock Vina produced PoseBusters-valid poses for essentially all three experimental ligands but retained only 50 to 60\% of generated poses after the study\textquotesingle s combined screen for on-receptor placement outside the transmembrane exclusion slab. Refined DiffDock was less consistent on validity yet retained more usable poses, with a median frame--ligand-unit physical-validity yield of 100\% and a median usable yield of 65\% on the same unit against 60\% for AutoDock Vina. EquiBind retained one pose out of 120 after validity and placement screening. These figures show how often each workflow satisfies the predefined admissibility criteria. They do not measure near-nativeness because no experimental ligand pose exists.

This distinction is especially important for the R91-E106 transmembrane exclusion slab. GSK-7975A sensitivity is linked directly or allosterically to selectivity-filter geometry \cite{ref070}, while Synta-66 has been mapped to an extracellular TM1/TM3 and loop region adjacent to that filter \cite{ref071}. Excluding the central conduction pathway is therefore a defensible operational choice for focusing on an outer-pore hypothesis, but it is not ground truth and cannot prove that every excluded pose is biologically wrong. The correct claim is that EquiBind produced almost no poses satisfying this study\textquotesingle s criterion of a valid, on-receptor pose outside the transmembrane exclusion slab.

The low cross-tool agreement reinforces the uncertainty. Consensus sites differed by a pooled median of 30.1 \angstrom{} on the experimental ligands, against 29.5 \angstrom{} on the control panel, but the experimental value rises to 42.2 \angstrom{} once each ligand is collapsed to the median over its four frames, so it is not a settled central estimate and the two libraries cannot be called equally scattered on this evidence. AutoDock Vina and DiffDock agreed within 5 \angstrom{} in none of the eleven evaluable experimental pairs, against 15\% of control-panel pairs, and no three-way experimental consensus occurred. AutoDock Vina formed the best-separated clouds in both ligand sets and the most compact ones on the control panel, whereas DiffDock was the tighter of the two on the experimental ligands at a median within-cluster spread of 0.34 against 0.62 \angstrom{}, but compactness measures precision rather than accuracy. A method can reproducibly sample the wrong site. Partial recovery of literature-associated residues near the selectivity filter and TM1/TM3 is therefore hypothesis-generating evidence, not validation of a bound pose. Combining one AutoDock contact with a different DiffDock contact likewise does not establish that either complete pose is correct.

One contrast between the three experimental ligands and the larger Orai control panel is established once the ligand rather than the frame-ligand pair is taken as the unit, namely AutoDock Vina\textquotesingle s lower usable yield on the experimental modulators, which survives correction at both units and on all three ligands. The three largest of the remaining gaps among the reference-free placement and clustering proxies, namely DiffDock\textquotesingle s higher physical-validity yield on the experimental modulators, its larger consensus-site distance from EquiBind and its lower silhouette on the experimental ligands, either lose their corrected significance under that collapse or rest on two experimental ligands, and the interaction-type family, chiefly the halogen-bond, hydrophobic, alkyl-\ensuremath{\pi} and hydrogen-bond differences between the two libraries, behaves the same way, with none of its contrasts surviving Benjamini-Hochberg correction at the ligand unit. The both-unit table of the ligand-level sensitivity analysis records this for every contrast, three of sixty reaching an adjusted p below 0.05 at the ligand unit against nineteen at the frame-ligand unit, with one of those three flagged as an artefact of the collapse. All of these comparisons are nonetheless reported descriptively, and the absence of a corrected difference elsewhere should not be described as proof of transfer or equivalence either. With three chemotypes, four correlated frames of one receptor and almost no surviving EquiBind output, the tests have limited power in both directions, and three ligands cannot support corrected inference either way. The preservation of tool ordering on physical validity is reassuring, but it does not extend to usable yield, where the experimental panel reverses the control-panel ordering, so it supports only cautious use of the control panel for these reference-free proxies. The four receptor frames also provide limited flexibility because three late snapshots are closely related. A stronger Orai study would add independent trajectories, systematic frame selection, explicit membrane and ion conditions where feasible, ligand-bound simulations and experimental tests such as mutagenesis or competition assays. Such experimental validation is the decisive test these reference-free proxies cannot supply, and it falls outside the scope of a computational thesis. It is being taken forward in continuing Orai1 research at the Johannes Kepler University Linz (JKU), to which the present work contributes the physically valid, on-receptor pose hypotheses generated here for the three modulators.

\hypertarget{computational-efficiency-and-practical-tool-selection}{%
\section{Computational Efficiency and Practical Tool Selection}\label{computational-efficiency-and-practical-tool-selection}}

Within the measured whole-protein configuration, EquiBind provided the shortest median elapsed cost per qualifying pose at 8.5 s, ahead of DiffDock at 50.9 s and AutoDock Vina + gnina at 119.7 s. EquiBind\textquotesingle s apparent advantage is conditional on the 62 of 302 timing-complete complexes where it succeeded, since it failed to produce a qualifying pose for the remaining 240, so DiffDock is the more realistic cost-effective choice at 168 successful complexes. Hardware-resource comparisons use different currencies. CPU-core-seconds cannot be ranked directly against GPU-seconds, and refinement is part of every pipeline\textquotesingle s cost here, the AutoDock arm included. The justified conclusion is therefore that, within the measured whole-protein protocol, the classical pipeline is the most expensive route to a qualifying pose on this hardware on both the wall-clock and hardware axes. Its apparent efficiency advantage in the earlier site-restricted configuration is associated with the different crystal-boxed protocol, not established as an intrinsic property of the search box or engine. What AutoDock Vina + gnina buys for that cost is coverage and physical admissibility, not speed.

Current work points toward hybrid rather than exclusive workflows. DiffDock-Glide combines pocket-conditioned diffusion with physics-based post-docking minimisation and reports improved sampling, particularly for targets without training-set homologues \cite{ref064}. PoseX similarly found that relaxation removed many AI steric failures \cite{ref012}. gnina 1.3 demonstrates that learned scoring can improve ranking within a classical sampler \cite{ref067}, while the prospective CACHE study shows that such a hybrid open-source workflow can produce experimental hits without establishing universal superiority \cite{ref068}. These studies align with the strongest finding of this thesis. Learned generation becomes more useful when followed by explicit physical validation and task-appropriate ranking.

\hypertarget{implications-for-docking-workflows}{%
\section{Implications for Docking Workflows}\label{implications-for-docking-workflows}}

The results support a staged workflow rather than a single universal engine, namely to generate multiple hypotheses, minimise them, reject physically invalid or biologically inadmissible structures, rank the survivors with a selector validated for the intended endpoint, and retain uncertainty when methods disagree. For the tools studied here, refined DiffDock and AutoDock Vina are best treated as complementary primary engines rather than a baseline and a supplement. DiffDock has higher point estimates for in-place accuracy and pocket localisation, although its near-nativeness advantage over AutoDock Vina is inconclusive at the tested ranking depths on the interval evidence given in the Results (Near-Nativeness and Form), while AutoDock Vina supplies the physically safer geometry. EquiBind contributes too little usable coverage in this configuration to justify routine inclusion. Its single usable Orai1 pose comes from the one arm that applied the in-protein Universal Force Field relaxation described in the Appendix, which deforms ligand geometry, so that figure understates what the method would deliver without that stage. The recommendation does not rest on it, since the relaxation-free Orai1 control panel still returns a usable yield of 2.9\% for EquiBind against 79.2\% for AutoDock Vina + gnina and 71.1\% for DiffDock + smina.

The next comparison should test whether this conclusion persists with current models under matched information and compute budgets. Flexible diffusion and packing methods \cite{ref031}, multi-pocket conditioning \cite{ref066}, hybrid refinement \cite{ref064}, modern co-folding systems \cite{ref012}, \cite{ref069} and strong template controls \cite{ref065} should be evaluated on protein-, pocket- and ligand-novel splits. The negative fpocket/P2Rank result for EquiBind also cautions that pocket detection alone is insufficient. The downstream docking model must use that information effectively. Overall, the evidence favours hybrid, flexibility-aware and leakage-controlled evaluation of the kind a standing blind community challenge provides \cite{ref047}, while preserving a strict distinction between benchmark accuracy and reference-free biological hypotheses.

\hypertarget{conclusions}{%
\chapter{\texorpdfstring{Conclusions }{Conclusions }}\label{conclusions}}

This thesis compared three configured docking pipelines on 303 crystallographic re-docking cases and on three Orai1 modulators docked against four receptor snapshots. The findings answer the four research questions as follows. The fourth, on the opportunities and limitations of the tested tools, is summarised here and developed in full in the Limitations and Future Outlook chapter.

\hypertarget{rq1-quality-and-stability}{%
\section{RQ1: Quality and Stability}\label{rq1-quality-and-stability}}

Under the headline metric, a PoseBusters-valid pose within 2 \angstrom{} of the crystal ligand, and with every pipeline searching the whole receptor blindly, DiffDock + smina performed best on point estimates, followed by AutoDock Vina + gnina and EquiBind + gnina. Approximate rank-1 recovery was 34.0\%, 29.4\% and 15.2\%, respectively, and the retrospective best-of-top-15 ceiling rose to 55.1\%, 49.8\% and 20.1\%. The DiffDock lead over AutoDock is not statistically separable at any depth on near-nativeness, the paired rank-1 difference being +4.6 percentage points with a 95\% interval of \ensuremath{-}2.2 to +11.4 points and the top-15 difference +5.3 points with an interval of \ensuremath{-}2.1 to +12.5. Those intervals order neither pipeline on this endpoint, and two one-sided tests bound the residual difference at about twelve percentage points in either direction, so the contrast is reported here as bounded rather than merely inconclusive. Both remain decisively ahead of EquiBind. The ordering inverts on physical validity, where pooled rates across the 303-complex accuracy set were 99.5\% for AutoDock Vina + gnina, 84.5\% for DiffDock + smina and 51.8\% for unguided EquiBind + gnina. Native-interaction recovery similarly leaves DiffDock + smina and AutoDock Vina + gnina not separable at a rank-1 F1 of 0.53 against 0.46 for EquiBind, a contrast with no detected paired difference on the 278 complexes the two share (Wilcoxon p = 0.69) and no equivalence test applied, so reported as an absence of detected difference rather than as equivalence, with DiffDock ahead only once the comparison is widened to the union of the top five poses (0.45 against 0.36 and 0.37 mean fingerprint overlap). On Orai1, the physical-validity ordering appeared again under reference-free proxies. Nearly every AutoDock pose was PoseBusters-valid, while the median frame--ligand-unit validity was 100\% for DiffDock and 5\% for EquiBind across the twelve experimental units. Usable yield ordered the two leading pipelines the other way. Median usable yields on the same unit under the study\textquotesingle s criterion of on-receptor placement outside the transmembrane exclusion slab were 65\% for DiffDock + smina, 60\% for AutoDock Vina + gnina and 0\% for EquiBind, with only one of 120 EquiBind poses surviving both validity and placement screening, on the one arm that carried the in-protein Universal Force Field relaxation. The relaxation-free control panel puts EquiBind at 2.9\% usable against 79.2\% and 71.1\%, so the ordering does not depend on that stage.

Stability was assessed only through static physical validity, clustering reproducibility and sensitivity to receptor frames. Thermodynamic or time-dependent stability of the docked complexes was not tested and cannot be inferred from docking alone.

\hypertarget{rq2-effect-of-post-docking-optimisation}{%
\section{RQ2: Effect of Post-Docking Optimisation}\label{rq2-effect-of-post-docking-optimisation}}

Local optimisation mainly repaired physical geometry rather than placement. DiffDock validity increased from 24.4\% in the raw output to 84.5\% with smina and 87.1\% with gnina. Unguided EquiBind increased from 2.9\% to 39.3\% with smina and 51.8\% with gnina. By contrast, optimisation changed the per-rank near-native rate (each pose held at its own rank) by only about 0.1 to 0.7 percentage points for DiffDock and 2.8 to 3.1 points for EquiBind with gnina (0.1 to 0.6 and 2.5 to 2.6 points, respectively, with smina). The larger cumulative top-\emph{k} gains in Table~\ref{tab:appendix-refinement-mcnemar} arise because the best-of-top-\emph{k} criterion accumulates these per-rank repairs across the ranked pool, and for EquiBind additionally because gnina re-ranks its otherwise unordered poses. Refinement is therefore necessary to make many AI-generated poses chemically and sterically usable, but it cannot recover an unsampled binding mode or relocate a grossly misplaced ligand.

\hypertarget{rq3-computational-efficiency}{%
\section{RQ3: Computational Efficiency}\label{rq3-computational-efficiency}}

The efficiency comparison is measured on the whole-protein search across 302 timing-complete complexes, with both stages of the AutoDock arm timed. EquiBind gave the shortest median wall-clock cost per qualifying pose at 8.5 s, compared with 50.9 s for DiffDock and 119.7 s for AutoDock Vina + gnina, and qualifying poses were obtained for 168 complexes with DiffDock, 151 with AutoDock Vina + gnina and only 62 with EquiBind. This reverses the ordering obtained under the crystal-boxed search, on which raw AutoDock was cheapest at 3.5 s. Three measured changes contribute, namely that the blind Vina search costs 2.3 times more per complex, the additional gnina rescoring pass costs a further five and a half times the search, and fewer complexes and poses meet the qualifying endpoint, enlarging the per-qualifying-pose denominator. Because the compared AutoDock pipelines and receptor preparations differ, the reversal is associated with the whole-protein protocol rather than attributable to the box alone. Hardware-resource accounting does not yield one universal ordering because CPU-core-seconds and GPU-seconds are different currencies, though on the whole-protein run AutoDock is the most expensive pipeline per qualifying pose on every axis at once, namely wall-clock, CPU-core-seconds and GPU-seconds. The efficiency result applies to these implementations and hardware settings rather than intrinsically to physics-based and AI-based docking as classes, and in particular the cost of the learned rescorer is an artefact of running it one pose at a time rather than of the method itself.

\hypertarget{rq4-opportunities-and-limitations}{%
\section{RQ4: Opportunities and Limitations}\label{rq4-opportunities-and-limitations}}

The opportunity lies in the fact that the three pipelines fail in different ways, so a staged workflow that generates candidates with a diffusion model, repairs their geometry by local minimisation, screens for physical validity and admissible placement and then ranks the survivors with a selector validated for the intended endpoint uses each component where it is strongest. The limitations are equally specific. Every result rests on configured toolchains at single settings without multi-seed sweeps, on a benchmark whose post-2021 cut-off narrows but does not remove similarity leakage, and on an Orai1 arm of three chemotypes and four correlated frames of one receptor without a cognate structure, so the reference-free proxies used there cannot establish which predicted pose is correct. Both sides of this answer are developed in the following chapter, which sets out the controls that would sharpen the comparison and the experimental evidence the Orai1 hypotheses still require.

Across these four questions, the overall picture is one of a workflow rather than a winner. For the datasets, tool versions, search settings and refiners tested here, AutoDock Vina produced the highest PoseBusters pass rate while DiffDock had the highest in-place accuracy point estimates, although the paired intervals leave its near-nativeness advantage over AutoDock Vina inconclusive at the tested ranking depths. Both were limited by pose selection to a similar degree, whereas EquiBind was limited chiefly by generation and localisation. The limitations that bound every one of these conclusions are set out in full in the following chapter.

\hypertarget{limitations-and-future-outlook}{%
\chapter{Limitations and Future Outlook}\label{limitations-and-future-outlook}}

The overall picture is narrower than a claim that physics beats AI, and narrower still than the reverse. Under a matched blind search AutoDock Vina gave the highest PoseBusters pass rate while DiffDock had higher in-place accuracy point estimates, without a statistically established near-nativeness advantage over AutoDock Vina at the tested ranking depths. Neither class therefore dominates once the classical engine loses its binding-site advantage. AutoDock Vina remains bound by rigid-receptor docking and empirical scoring. DiffDock was selection-limited, as its confidence ranking failed to surface many credible poses. EquiBind was limited in generation, localisation and geometry. For Orai1, Vina and refined DiffDock can support hypotheses but establish no native pose. The most defensible workflow is hybrid, namely to generate candidates, filter for physical validity and biological placement, rank with a crystal-blind selector, and test the final hypotheses against independent evidence.

DiffDock stochasticity also limits exact regeneration. The 303-complex calibration run predates the local inference-seed patch and is a single unseeded draw, so rerunning that arm may produce a different pose sample even under the same documented settings. The Orai1 experimental panel is distinct, because it was generated after the seed support was added and uses seed 42, so that panel can be repeated exactly. The Orai1 control panel carries no recorded seed (Table~\ref{tab:appendix-orai-protocols}) and is therefore no more exactly reproducible than the calibration run, which matters because the control panel supplies most of the Orai1 DiffDock numbers reported here.

These conclusions are bounded. The results describe configured toolchains, thirty EquiBind conformers with gnina refining and ranking, at single settings without multi-seed sweeps, so physics-versus-AI labels oversimplify Vina\textquotesingle s empirical and gnina\textquotesingle s learned scoring. The dominant variant of each toolchain was also chosen on the same 303 complexes on which it is then scored, with no held-out split between selection and evaluation, so every calibration rate quoted in this chapter and in the Results is optimistic as an estimate of performance on unseen targets. The Orai1 DiffDock arm carries a further caveat of its own, namely the seeding asymmetry set out above, under which the experimental panel can be repeated exactly while the control panel carries no recorded seed. Two defects were also corrected before those results were generated. Graphics-memory was not released between complexes, which made the later complexes of a batch fail without a readable error and left both GSK-7975A forms without poses, and the output directories accumulated poses across runs, so earlier analyses drew on unequal and partly stale pose sets. Every Orai1 DiffDock number on the experimental panel comes from a single run in which each frame-ligand unit contributes thirty poses, capped at ten for analysis. The control panel is not on that footing. It was sampled at ten poses per unit rather than thirty and fell short of even that cap on 178 of its 1,215 units, returning 11,673 poses in all, so its per-unit yields rest on a unit-varying denominator. A post-2021 release does not remove leakage. The search is now matched, because all three pipelines were given the whole receptor, so the earlier crystal-centred-box asymmetry no longer qualifies the calibration result, but a blind search is also a harder task than the cognate re-docking regime in which AutoDock Vina is usually benchmarked, and its absolute numbers here should not be compared with published re-docking rates. Poses from one complex, and four correlated frames spanning three chemotypes, are not independent, so the complex or trajectory is the inferential unit, and non-significant contrasts are not evidence of equivalence. Best-of-top-k and crystal-nearest-cluster results are oracle ceilings, not deployable rank-1 accuracy, and because gnina both optimised and ranked EquiBind, the DiffDock + smina and gnina variants must stay named. PoseBusters checks only gross geometry, not free energy, protonation, solvent or membrane stability, and no ligand-bound dynamics were run. Orai1 offers one model, four frames and three cognate-free ligands, and its transmembrane slab is an operational hypothesis. The Orai1 AutoDock arm is gnina-rescored to match the calibration benchmark, and that rescoring covers 1,201 of the 1,215 analysable control-panel units, the fourteen missing units being macrocyclic ligands whose rescored poses could not be rebuilt to a topology matching their input, so the control panel is marginally smaller than the raw search on that arm. The DiffDock arm covers all 1,215 units but does not always fill the ten-pose cap, returning 11,673 control-panel poses against 12,010 for AutoDock Vina over its 1,201 units and 12,150 for EquiBind, so per-unit yields on that arm rest on a slightly smaller and unit-varying pose count. Because GSK-7975A and Synta-66 act near the pore \cite{ref070}, \cite{ref071}, a usable pose is not necessarily correct. CPU-core-seconds and GPU-seconds are never summed, and cost per qualifying pose is conditional on success, which flatters EquiBind.

The asymmetric search was the most tractable of these limitations, and it has been removed. Because AutoDock Vina requires a search box while DiffDock and EquiBind localise the ligand themselves, an equal-information comparison means withdrawing Vina\textquotesingle s crystal-centred advantage rather than granting the learned tools a pocket, which is the design adopted here and the convention that current blind-docking benchmarks assume \cite{ref069}, \cite{ref061}. The result is informative because it did not preserve the earlier ordering. In the like-for-like raw Vina arms, validity-aware rank-1 recovery falls from 55.4\% (168 of 303) in the superseded crystal-centred run to 25.7\% (78 of 303) in the blind run. The selected blind AutoDock Vina + gnina arm reaches 29.4\% (89 of 303). DiffDock, which was blind in both analyses, moves from 32.3\% to 34.0\% on the reported endpoint. These cross-run contrasts associate the whole-protein protocol with the reversal, but they do not isolate the search box itself, because receptor preparation also differs between the runs and the AutoDock treatment must be held fixed for a like-for-like comparison. The physical-validity advantage does persist in the blind analysis, but attributing it to a particular stage remains an interpretation because the design does not separate search, scoring and local optimisation.

Two further controls would sharpen this. The first is an intermediate arm in which AutoDock Vina receives a predicted rather than a crystallographic pocket, for instance the end-to-end P2Rank-Vina pipeline used by PoseBench \cite{ref069}, \cite{ref061}, which would measure how much of the lost ground a realistic site predictor recovers and is the configuration a practitioner would actually deploy. The second is a search-effort control, because exhaustiveness was held at 32 while the search volume grew by orders of magnitude, and box volume and search effort are known to interact \cite{ref079}, \cite{ref081}. Part of the measured drop is therefore attributable to under-sampling a larger space rather than to the scoring function, and separating the two requires re-running the whole-protein arm at higher exhaustiveness. Until that is done the blind AutoDock numbers should be read as a lower bound on what the engine can do without site information.

Several directions already exist and should be benchmarked directly, not treated as speculation, namely flexible side-chain diffusion \cite{ref031}, co-folding evaluated in PoseX and PoseBench \cite{ref012}, \cite{ref069}, DiffDock-Glide \cite{ref064}, PocketVina \cite{ref066}, updated gnina scoring \cite{ref067} and template controls such as TEMPL \cite{ref065}. Because pose accuracy alone is insufficient \cite{ref032}, the strongest near-term workflow is that hybrid pipeline, evaluated against current models, not the versions tested here.

For Orai1 in particular, the poses reported here are computational hypotheses rather than validated complexes, and the reference-free proxies used to rank them cannot establish which, if any, is correct. Settling that question requires wet-lab evidence, namely site-directed mutagenesis, competition or displacement assays and, ideally, a ligand-bound structure, which no docking study can provide on its own. This experimental validation is being pursued in continuing research at JKU, whose Orai1 modulators motivated the reference-free case study, and the present thesis contributes to that programme the physically screened, on-receptor pose hypotheses together with the tool-selection evidence on which such follow-up can build. The lasting contribution of the reference-free arm is therefore not a bound pose but a documented and physically screened workflow for generating and filtering testable Orai1 hypotheses, and a defensible ordering of the tools that produce them.
